TCP — state machine, hashes, drain, recv, retransmit
====================================================

The full TCP/IPv4 implementation lives here as one sibling
because the state-machine ↔ drain ↔ retransmit ↔ recv ↔
hashes web is too tightly knit to split further without
introducing artificial cycles. Sections within (each marked
with a `### ` heading in the prose):

  - state machine — slot table, status enums, lifecycle
  - connected-socket hash (Phase 3 chunk 3) — chained by
    `(local_port ^ remote_port ^ low16(remote_ip)) & 0xf`
  - listen-socket hash (Phase 3 chunk 4) — chained by
    `local_port & 0xf`; disjoint from the conn hash
  - owners ring — multi-tid socket ownership, per-tid
    quotas, transplant accounting
  - TCP option parsers — SACK, Window Scale (RFC 7323 §2),
    Timestamps (RFC 7323 §3)
  - SACK scoreboard (RFC 2018) — coalescing merge, advance-
    on-cumulative-ACK, "next un-SACKed byte" walker
  - PMTU-driven effective MSS + segment build
  - retransmit + congestion (RFC 5681) — cwnd growth on
    ACK, RTO collapse, fast-retransmit/fast-recovery
  - drain pump — sender side, segments out of `send_buf`
  - inbound dispatch — parse, validate, state-step
  - reorder queue + reassembly to user recv_buf
  - close paths — slot cleanup, owner-exit reclaim, RST
  - selftest helpers (test-only)

Same `oskit.servers` module as `inet_proto.lsysl`, so all the
shared state — ARP cache, frame pool, IP/MAC vars, link-layer
helpers, ICMP builders — resolves as siblings without imports.

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.ipc.{ipc_reply, ipc_reply_handler}
    import oskit.services.{monotonic_ms}
    import std.alloc.{malloc, free}
    import std.net.{ETHERTYPE_IPV4, IP_PROTO_TCP,
        pkt_read_u16, pkt_write_u16, pkt_write_mac, pkt_write_ipv4,
        net_checksum, ipv4, ipv4_octet}


### TCP — state machine

Full state machine for active and passive opens, graceful close
from either side, real TIME_WAIT (2*MSL), out-of-order receive
reassembly (bounded reorder queue), Reno congestion control with
SACK-assisted fast recovery, and RFC 6298 RTO estimation
(SRTT/RTTVAR). Exposes both blocking and non-blocking recv, half-
close via FIN-without-close, and owner-keyed cleanup on process
exit. Implemented in cooperation with `inet.lsysl` — inet.lsysl
hosts the IPC dispatch, the heavy lifting (state transitions,
emit/retransmit, scoreboard) lives here.

Tick budget assumes a ~100 Hz scheduler timer (10 ms per tick).

    val TCP_MAX_SOCKETS     = 16
    // Default heap-backed buffer sizes per socket. Allocated lazily
    // in `inet_tcp_alloc` and freed in `inet_tcp_close_slot`. The
    // per-socket size is recorded in `send_buf_size` / `recv_buf_size`
    // so future setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF) / setsockopt(SO_RCVBUF) work
    // can vary the size without re-running alloc.
    val TCP_SEND_BUF_DEFAULT = 4096
    val TCP_RECV_BUF_DEFAULT = 1024
    // setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF/SO_RCVBUF) clamps. Min keeps a useful
    // amount of data flowing; max is bounded by the inet server's
    // heap budget (post-C3 heap on x86 server.ld is ~395 KB; with
    // 16 sockets at 8 KB+8 KB worst case that's 256 KB, comfortable).
    val TCP_BUF_MIN          = 256
    val TCP_BUF_MAX          = 8192
    // Phase 2 chunk 2 — per-tid TCP buffer memory cap. Sum of
    // send_buf_size + recv_buf_size for every in-use TCP slot
    // owned by a single tid is held under this ceiling. 64 KB is
    // ~1.6 × the all-default footprint of 8 sockets (8 × 5120 =
    // 40 KB) — comfortable for the per-tid socket cap (chunk 1)
    // while leaving room for setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF/SO_RCVBUF)
    // grows. Caller-visible behaviour: alloc paths refuse with
    // -EMFILE, resize_buf silently clamps. Companion to
    // `inet_count_tcp_owner` for the slot-count cap.
    val TCP_PER_TID_BUF_MAX  = 65536
    // SO_KEEPALIVE timing in milliseconds (`monotonic_ms` clock).
    // Linux defaults are 7200/75/9 (seconds) — too long to exercise
    // in tests.  Slix picks tighter defaults so the probe/giveup
    // cycle is observable in seconds rather than hours.  Real
    // workloads can tune later via setsockopt(TCP_KEEPIDLE / etc.)
    // once those options are wired (deferred follow-up).
    val TCP_KEEPALIVE_IDLE_MS    = 10000  // 10s of silence before first probe
    val TCP_KEEPALIVE_INTVL_MS   = 5000   // 5s between probes
    val TCP_KEEPALIVE_MAX_PROBES = 3      // close after this many unanswered
    val TCP_MSS_ADV         = 1460
    val TCP_MAX_SEG         = 480
    // RTO bounds + initial value, all in `monotonic_ms` milliseconds.
    val TCP_RTO_MS_INITIAL  = 1000
    val TCP_RTO_MS_MIN      = 200
    val TCP_RTO_MS_MAX      = 60000
    // 2*MSL ≈ 2s on slix (RFC 793 leaves MSL implementation-defined).
    val TCP_TW_MS           = 2000
    // FIN_WAIT_2 idle close — Linux's default tcp_fin_timeout is 60s.
    val TCP_FW2_MS          = 60000
    val TCP_MAX_RETRIES     = 5
    val TCP_REORDER_SLOTS   = 4

    // Congestion control (RFC 5681 + RFC 6928). Initial cwnd of
    // 10 × SMSS matches the modern "IW10" recommendation from
    // RFC 6928 §2 — Linux's default since ~2011. Gives enough
    // in-flight room to pipeline a handful of segments in the
    // first RTT so dup-ACK-driven loss detection (fast retransmit
    // / fast recovery) has segments to work with.
    //
    // SSthresh starts at a large value so we stay in slow start
    // until the first loss or our cwnd actually catches up to a
    // reasonable estimate of bandwidth-delay product.
    val TCP_INIT_CWND       = 4800
    val TCP_INIT_SSTHRESH   = 65535
    val TCP_MIN_CWND        = 480

    // TCP option kinds (RFC 793, RFC 2018, RFC 7323).
    val TCP_OPT_EOL             = 0
    val TCP_OPT_NOP             = 1
    val TCP_OPT_MSS             = 2
    val TCP_OPT_WSCALE          = 3
    val TCP_OPT_SACK_PERMITTED  = 4
    val TCP_OPT_SACK            = 5
    val TCP_OPT_TS              = 8

    // Receive-window scale shift we always advertise. With the
    // refactor-only C3 default (`TCP_RECV_BUF_DEFAULT=1024`) the
    // shift granularity (128 B per advert) is coarser than the
    // buffer itself — silly window kicks in early. Once setsockopt
    // can grow the buffer, scale=7 (× 128) lets a 256 KB recv_buf
    // advertise up through 32 MB of window, well above the 16-bit
    // raw field.
    val TCP_RCV_WSCALE          = 7

    // Max SACK blocks we bother with on either side. RFC 2018
    // technically allows up to 4 in a 40-byte option space; we
    // cap at 3 so the option (26 bytes) + 2 NOPs for 4-byte
    // alignment (28 bytes) fits comfortably alongside any other
    // options we might carry on an ACK.
    val TCP_MAX_SACK_BLOCKS     = 3

    val TCP_STATE_CLOSED      = 0
    val TCP_STATE_SYN_SENT    = 1
    val TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED = 2
    val TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_1  = 3
    val TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_2  = 4
    val TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT  = 5
    val TCP_STATE_LAST_ACK    = 6
    val TCP_STATE_TIME_WAIT   = 7
    val TCP_STATE_LISTEN      = 8
    val TCP_STATE_SYN_RCVD    = 9

    val TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG = 16

    val TCP_F_FIN = 0x01
    val TCP_F_SYN = 0x02
    val TCP_F_RST = 0x04
    val TCP_F_PSH = 0x08
    val TCP_F_ACK = 0x10

    struct TcpReorderEntry
        in_use: int
        seq: u32
        len: int
        data: [480]byte

    // RFC 2018 §3: a SACK block is `[left, right)` describing a
    // contiguous byte range the peer buffered out-of-order.
    // `TCP_SACK_BOARD` caps the per-socket scoreboard we
    // accumulate across ACKs during a single recovery round.
    struct TcpSackBlock
        in_use: int
        left: u32
        right: u32

    val TCP_SACK_BOARD = 8

    val INET_OWNERS_MAX = 4

    struct TcpSocket
        in_use: int
        state: int
        owners: [4]int
        owners_count: int
        // Phase 2B chunk 3: in-flight SCM_RIGHTS references. See
        // InetSocket.queued_refs in inet.lsysl for the contract.
        queued_refs: int
        local_port: int
        remote_ip: u32
        remote_port: int
        iss: u32
        irs: u32
        snd_una: u32
        snd_nxt: u32
        snd_wnd: int
        rcv_nxt: u32
        mss: int
        // Heap-backed send/recv buffers. `*_ptr` is null when the
        // slot is free (in_use == 0). `*_size` records the alloc
        // size so flow-control math (rwnd advert, send-buf space)
        // works against the per-socket capacity.
        send_buf_ptr: *byte
        send_buf_size: int
        send_len: int
        recv_buf_ptr: *byte
        recv_buf_size: int
        recv_len: int
        rtx_deadline: int
        retries: int
        tw_deadline: int
        peer_fin: int
        our_fin_sent: int
        connect_waiter: int
        send_waiter: int
        send_waiter_len: int
        send_deadline: int

`send_deadline` is the SO_SNDTIMEO deadline in `monotonic_ms`
milliseconds.  0 means "park forever" (Linux default). Set when a
TCP_SEND arrives carrying a non-zero `timeout_ms` and parks; the
timer scan replies `(status=2, n=0)` when `now >= deadline`.

`user_timeout_ms` and `earliest_unack_ms` together drive
RFC 5482 TCP_USER_TIMEOUT — a per-connection cap on how long the
socket may remain unable to drain its unacked window. Distinct from
SO_SNDTIMEO: USER_TIMEOUT survives an unblocked send and governs
the connection itself; SNDTIMEO governs only the current send
call's blocking window.

  - `user_timeout_ms` is the configured cap, in milliseconds.
    0 disables — slix has no sysctl for the system default, so 0
    means "no cap".
  - `earliest_unack_ms` is the absolute `monotonic_ms` timestamp
    at which the *current* unacked run started.  Set in `inet_tcp_do_send` when
    `send_len` transitions 0 → >0; reset to 0 in the ACK path when
    `snd_una` reaches `snd_nxt` (everything drained); restarted on
    partial cumulative ACKs so the countdown is anchored on the
    most recent cumulative-ACK boundary (RFC 5482 §3, "any progress
    restarts the timer").

        user_timeout_ms: int
        earliest_unack_ms: int

`ack_blackholed` is a test-only override: when nonzero,
`inet_tcp_emit` suppresses pure-ACK segments (ACK-only, no payload,
no SYN/FIN/RST) so the peer sees no acknowledgements while
SYN/FIN/RST handshake bytes still flow. Lets a single-process
loopback test simulate "peer went dark mid-conversation" for
TCP_USER_TIMEOUT verification. Set via `INET_CMD_TCP_BLACKHOLE_ACKS_TEST`
(53); cleared on `close_slot`.

        ack_blackholed: int
        recv_waiter: int
        recv_maxlen: int
        close_waiter: int
        is_listen: int
        accept_queue: [16]int
        accept_head: int
        accept_tail: int
        accept_count: int
        // Max queue depth requested by listen()'s `backlog` arg.
        // Capped to TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG (the array size). 0 is
        // treated as 1 — Linux's "negative or zero → 1" rule.
        accept_backlog: int
        accept_waiter: int
        parent_listen: int
        reorder: [4]TcpReorderEntry
        fin_seq: u32
        fin_pending: int
        srtt: int
        rttvar: int
        rto: int
        rtt_measured: int
        rtt_sample_valid: int
        rtt_sample_seq: u32
        rtt_sample_time: int
        snd_cwnd: int
        snd_ssthresh: int
        dup_ack_count: int
        fast_recovery: int
        recovery_point: u32
        sack_ok: int
        sack_board: [8]TcpSackBlock
        sack_rexmit_cursor: u32
        sack_rexmit_valid: int
        rexmit_count: int
        // Per-fd fire counter for TCP — bumped on every readiness
        // edge by `inet_tcp_bump_fire_seq` (called from
        // `inet_epoll_fire`), returned to the shim by
        // `inet_handle_poll` so EPOLLET tracks per-fd edges.
        fire_seq: int
        // RFC 7323 §2 window scale (negotiated on the SYN
        // exchange). `snd_wscale` is the shift the peer announced
        // — every inbound non-SYN window field gets `<< snd_wscale`
        // before storing in `snd_wnd`. `rcv_wscale` is the shift
        // *we* announce — every outbound non-SYN window field gets
        // `>> rcv_wscale` before writing.  Both default to 0 when
        // either side fails to send the WS option.
        snd_wscale: int
        rcv_wscale: int
        // RFC 7323 §3 timestamps. `ts_ok` is set iff both sides
        // advertised TS in the SYN exchange — only then do we
        // include TS on subsequent segments. `ts_recent` is the
        // last in-window TSval we've seen from the peer; we echo
        // it as TSecr on every outbound segment. `ts_offset` is a
        // per-connection bias added to our outbound TSval so the
        // raw kernel tick count doesn't leak (and so RTT math
        // stays consistent: RTT = now - tsecr + ts_offset).
        ts_ok: int
        ts_recent: u32
        ts_offset: u32
        // SO_KEEPALIVE state.  `keepalive_on != 0` activates the
        // probe/giveup cycle.  `last_recv_ms` is the absolute
        // `monotonic_ms` timestamp of the most recent inbound
        // segment — the idle-timer baseline.  `keepalive_probes`
        // counts unanswered probes; `keepalive_next_probe` is the
        // absolute `monotonic_ms` timestamp at which the next probe
        // (or first probe after idle) fires (0 = none scheduled,
        // recomputed lazily in scan_timers).
        keepalive_on: int
        last_recv_ms: int
        keepalive_probes: int
        keepalive_next_probe: int
        // Per-socket keepalive overrides (TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL
        // / TCP_KEEPCNT). 0 = use the global TCP_KEEPALIVE_* default.
        // Idle/intvl are stored as ms (already converted from
        // seconds at the shim boundary, `secs * 1000`); cnt is the
        // probe-giveup threshold.
        keepalive_idle: int
        keepalive_intvl: int
        keepalive_cnt: int
        // Asynchronous-error channel for SO_ERROR.  Holds the positive
        // errno of a connection-level failure (RST in SYN_SENT/SYN_RCVD,
        // post-handshake RST, retransmit/keepalive timeout) so that
        // `getsockopt(SO_ERROR)` can surface it after `close_slot` has
        // already torn the slot down — the slot's `in_use` is cleared
        // *before* the EPOLL fire so the shim's wake-poll sees mask=0,
        // and a separate field (read-and-cleared via the
        // `INET_CMD_TCP_GET_ERR` IPC) is the only way to recover the
        // reason. Survives `close_slot`; reset by `inet_tcp_alloc`.
        pending_error: int
        // Set by `inet_tcp_emit` when the next-hop MAC isn't in the
        // ARP cache; cleared (and the socket retransmitted) by
        // `inet_arp_drain_pending` when the matching ARP reply
        // lands.  Without this we waste an RTO on the first SYN to
        // any off-cache peer; the retx-on-drain path saves that RTT.
        arp_pending: int
        // Phase 3 chunk 3: forward link in the TCP-connected 4-tuple
        // hash bucket. -1 = not in any bucket. Maintained by
        // `inet_tcp_conn_hash_insert` / `inet_tcp_conn_hash_remove`
        // whenever a slot transitions in/out of having a full
        // (local_port, remote_port, remote_ip) tuple.
        next_in_tcp_conn_bucket: int
        // Phase 3 chunk 4: forward link in the listen-port hash
        // bucket. -1 = not in any bucket. Disjoint from the conn
        // bucket: a slot is in at most one of the two hashes
        // depending on `is_listen`.
        next_in_tcp_listen_bucket: int

    var tcp_sockets: [16]TcpSocket
    var tcp_iss_counter: u32 = u32(1000)


### TCP connected-socket hash (Phase 3 chunk 3)

`inet_tcp_find` is called once per inbound TCP segment to map an
ingress 4-tuple to a slot index. The pre-existing implementation
linearly scanned all 16 slots even when 15 of them were unrelated
sockets in different states. The hash chains by
`(local_port ^ remote_port ^ low16(remote_ip)) & 0xf`, so the per-
packet cost is amortised constant regardless of how many sockets
are open.

LISTEN sockets stay out of this hash — they have no remote tuple.
Phase 3 chunk 4 introduces a separate listen hash keyed on
`local_port` alone.

Maintenance: insert when a slot transitions to having the full
4-tuple set (active connect path in `inet_tcp_begin_connect`,
passive accept path in `inet_tcp_accept_child`); remove in
`inet_tcp_close_slot`. The remove is a safe no-op for slots never
inserted (listen sockets, fresh-alloc-then-immediately-close-slot
paths), so the close handler can call it unconditionally.

    val INET_TCP_HASH_N = 16

    var inet_tcp_conn_hash: [16]int

    inet_tcp_conn_bucket(local_port: int, remote_port: int, remote_ip: u32) -> int
        ((local_port ^ remote_port ^ int(remote_ip & u32(0xffff))) & (INET_TCP_HASH_N - 1))

    inet_tcp_conn_hash_init()
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_TCP_HASH_N
            inet_tcp_conn_hash[i] = -1
            i += 1

    inet_tcp_conn_hash_insert(s: int)
        val h = inet_tcp_conn_bucket(tcp_sockets[s].local_port,
                                      tcp_sockets[s].remote_port,
                                      tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip)
        tcp_sockets[s].next_in_tcp_conn_bucket = inet_tcp_conn_hash[h]
        inet_tcp_conn_hash[h] = s

    inet_tcp_conn_hash_remove(s: int)
        val h = inet_tcp_conn_bucket(tcp_sockets[s].local_port,
                                      tcp_sockets[s].remote_port,
                                      tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip)
        var cur = inet_tcp_conn_hash[h]
        if cur == s
            inet_tcp_conn_hash[h] = tcp_sockets[s].next_in_tcp_conn_bucket
            tcp_sockets[s].next_in_tcp_conn_bucket = -1
            return
        while cur >= 0
            val nx = tcp_sockets[cur].next_in_tcp_conn_bucket
            if nx == s
                tcp_sockets[cur].next_in_tcp_conn_bucket = tcp_sockets[s].next_in_tcp_conn_bucket
                tcp_sockets[s].next_in_tcp_conn_bucket = -1
                return
            cur = nx


### TCP listen-socket hash (Phase 3 chunk 4)

`inet_tcp_find_listen` is called once per inbound TCP segment that
fails the connected-socket lookup (typically: a SYN to a listening
port). The pre-existing implementation scanned every slot looking
for `is_listen != 0 && local_port == p`. With this hash a SYN is
mapped to its listener via a single masked index instead.

Same chained-list shape as the connected-socket hash (chunk 3) but
disjoint storage and a different key (just `local_port`, since a
listener has no remote tuple). A slot is in at most one of the two
hashes — a listener is never in the conn hash, and a connected slot
is never in the listen hash.

Maintenance: insert in `inet_tcp_begin_listen` after `is_listen = 1`;
remove in `inet_tcp_close_slot` (gated on `is_listen != 0`). The
listen-vs-conn discriminator at close time avoids unnecessary walks.

    var inet_tcp_listen_hash: [16]int

    inet_tcp_listen_bucket(local_port: int) -> int
        (local_port & 0xffff) & (INET_TCP_HASH_N - 1)

    inet_tcp_listen_hash_init()
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_TCP_HASH_N
            inet_tcp_listen_hash[i] = -1
            i += 1

    inet_tcp_listen_hash_insert(s: int)
        val h = inet_tcp_listen_bucket(tcp_sockets[s].local_port)
        tcp_sockets[s].next_in_tcp_listen_bucket = inet_tcp_listen_hash[h]
        inet_tcp_listen_hash[h] = s

    inet_tcp_listen_hash_remove(s: int)
        val h = inet_tcp_listen_bucket(tcp_sockets[s].local_port)
        var cur = inet_tcp_listen_hash[h]
        if cur == s
            inet_tcp_listen_hash[h] = tcp_sockets[s].next_in_tcp_listen_bucket
            tcp_sockets[s].next_in_tcp_listen_bucket = -1
            return
        while cur >= 0
            val nx = tcp_sockets[cur].next_in_tcp_listen_bucket
            if nx == s
                tcp_sockets[cur].next_in_tcp_listen_bucket = tcp_sockets[s].next_in_tcp_listen_bucket
                tcp_sockets[s].next_in_tcp_listen_bucket = -1
                return
            cur = nx

    // Bump the per-TCP-fd fire counter. Defined here, after the
    // TcpSocket declaration, so `inet_epoll_fire` (further up in
    // this file) can call it as a forward function reference —
    // the analyzer handles forward fn refs but not forward var refs.
    inet_tcp_bump_fire_seq(s: int)
        if s >= 0 && s < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            tcp_sockets[s].fire_seq = tcp_sockets[s].fire_seq + 1


Owners-ring helpers for `tcp_sockets[s]`. Mirrors the unix-socket
shape (UNIX_MAX_OWNERS): up to `INET_OWNERS_MAX` tids per slot, the
live entries occupy the prefix `[0..owners_count)` and -1 elsewhere.
The primary owner sits at `owners[0]` — that's the tid we charge
the per-tid slot/buffer budget against (Phase 2 chunk 2). Add/remove
preserve `owners[0]` identity for budgeting consistency: removing
the primary owner shifts the array down so the next owner becomes
primary.

    inet_tcp_owners_init(s: int)
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_OWNERS_MAX
            tcp_sockets[s].owners[i] = -1
            i += 1
        tcp_sockets[s].owners_count = 0

    inet_tcp_owners_set(s: int, tid: int)
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_OWNERS_MAX
            tcp_sockets[s].owners[i] = -1
            i += 1
        tcp_sockets[s].owners[0] = tid
        tcp_sockets[s].owners_count = 1

    inet_tcp_owners_primary(s: int) -> int
        if tcp_sockets[s].owners_count == 0
            return -1
        tcp_sockets[s].owners[0]

    inet_tcp_owners_has(s: int, tid: int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < tcp_sockets[s].owners_count
            if tcp_sockets[s].owners[i] == tid
                return 1
            i += 1
        0

    inet_tcp_owners_add(s: int, tid: int) -> int
        if inet_tcp_owners_has(s, tid) != 0
            return 1
        if tcp_sockets[s].owners_count >= INET_OWNERS_MAX
            return 0
        tcp_sockets[s].owners[tcp_sockets[s].owners_count] = tid
        tcp_sockets[s].owners_count += 1
        1

    inet_tcp_owners_remove(s: int, tid: int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < tcp_sockets[s].owners_count
            if tcp_sockets[s].owners[i] == tid
                var j = i
                while j < tcp_sockets[s].owners_count - 1
                    tcp_sockets[s].owners[j] = tcp_sockets[s].owners[j + 1]
                    j += 1
                tcp_sockets[s].owners_count -= 1
                // Don't wipe the trailing slot when count drops to
                // 0 — `inet_handle_tcp_get_err` reads `owners[0]` to
                // gate SO_ERROR access in the post-close window
                // before alloc reuses the slot. For count > 0 we
                // still wipe so the prefix-only invariant holds.
                if tcp_sockets[s].owners_count > 0
                    tcp_sockets[s].owners[tcp_sockets[s].owners_count] = -1
                return tcp_sockets[s].owners_count
            i += 1
        tcp_sockets[s].owners_count

    inet_tcp_owners_was_last(s: int, tid: int) -> int
        // True iff `tid` is the slot's primary owner — even when
        // `owners_count == 0` (slot already torn down). Used by
        // `inet_handle_tcp_get_err` to gate SO_ERROR after close.
        if tcp_sockets[s].owners[0] == tid
            return 1
        0

    tcp_next_iss() -> u32
        val v = tcp_iss_counter
        tcp_iss_counter = v + u32(65536)
        v


Walk TCP sockets that recorded `arp_pending=1` and whose
next-hop matches the freshly-cached `ip`.  Clear the flag and
re-emit via `inet_tcp_retransmit` so the first SYN/segment can
go out immediately instead of waiting an RTO.  Defined here
because `tcp_sockets` is a forward-var reference from
`inet_arp_drain_pending` higher up — the analyzer accepts a
forward fn ref but not a forward var ref.

    inet_tcp_arp_drain(ip: u32)
        var ti = 0
        while ti < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            if tcp_sockets[ti].in_use != 0
                if tcp_sockets[ti].arp_pending != 0
                    var next_hop = tcp_sockets[ti].remote_ip
                    if inet_in_subnet(next_hop) == 0
                        next_hop = ipv4(INET_GW_IP_A, INET_GW_IP_B,
                            INET_GW_IP_C, INET_GW_IP_D)
                    if next_hop == ip
                        tcp_sockets[ti].arp_pending = 0
                        inet_tcp_retransmit(ti)
            ti += 1


Phase 2 of the net-stack quality plan: count TCP slots that
include `tid` in their owners ring. Companion to
`inet_count_udp_owner` in inet.lsysl; callers add the two for the
full per-tid usage. Lives in this file because cross-file VAR
reads break the analyzer (see feedback_sysl_syntax_gotchas #19);
cross-file FN calls are fine, hence the split.

Multi-owner ramification: a slot transplanted from A to B counts
against *both* A's and B's per-tid quotas until A closes its
fd. Conservative; the alternative (count only the primary owner)
would let post-transplant code outrun B's budget.

    inet_count_tcp_owner(tid: int) -> int
        var n = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_tcp_owners_has(i, tid) != 0
                    n += 1
            i += 1
        n


Phase 2 chunk 2 — sum the heap-backed send_buf_size +
recv_buf_size of every in-use slot that includes `tid` in its
owners ring. Used by `inet_tcp_resize_buf` to clamp
setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF/SO_RCVBUF), and by alloc-paths
(`inet_tcp_begin_connect`, `inet_tcp_begin_listen`,
`inet_tcp_handle_passive_syn`) to gate new sockets against the
per-tid `TCP_PER_TID_BUF_MAX`.

    inet_tcp_buf_bytes_owner(tid: int) -> int
        var n = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_tcp_owners_has(i, tid) != 0
                    n += tcp_sockets[i].send_buf_size
                    n += tcp_sockets[i].recv_buf_size
            i += 1
        n


    inet_tcp_init()
        inet_tcp_conn_hash_init()
        inet_tcp_listen_hash_init()
        var i = 0
        while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            tcp_sockets[i].in_use = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].state = TCP_STATE_CLOSED
            tcp_sockets[i].next_in_tcp_conn_bucket = -1
            tcp_sockets[i].next_in_tcp_listen_bucket = -1
            tcp_sockets[i].connect_waiter = -1
            tcp_sockets[i].send_waiter = -1
            tcp_sockets[i].send_deadline = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].user_timeout_ms = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].earliest_unack_ms = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].ack_blackholed = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].recv_waiter = -1
            tcp_sockets[i].close_waiter = -1
            tcp_sockets[i].is_listen = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].accept_head = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].accept_tail = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].accept_count = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].accept_backlog = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].accept_waiter = -1
            tcp_sockets[i].parent_listen = -1
            tcp_sockets[i].fin_pending = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].srtt = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].rttvar = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].rto = TCP_RTO_MS_INITIAL
            tcp_sockets[i].rtt_measured = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].rtt_sample_valid = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].snd_cwnd = TCP_INIT_CWND
            tcp_sockets[i].snd_ssthresh = TCP_INIT_SSTHRESH
            tcp_sockets[i].dup_ack_count = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].fast_recovery = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].recovery_point = u32(0)
            tcp_sockets[i].sack_ok = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].sack_rexmit_valid = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].rexmit_count = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].fire_seq = 0
            tcp_sockets[i].queued_refs = 0
            var k = 0
            while k < TCP_REORDER_SLOTS
                tcp_sockets[i].reorder[k].in_use = 0
                k += 1
            var bi = 0
            while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
                tcp_sockets[i].sack_board[bi].in_use = 0
                bi += 1
            i += 1


32-bit sequence-number comparisons, wrapping modulo 2^32.

    tcp_seq_lt(a: u32, b: u32) -> int
        val d = i32(a - b)
        if d < 0
            return 1
        0

    tcp_seq_le(a: u32, b: u32) -> int
        val d = i32(a - b)
        if d <= 0
            return 1
        0


TCP seq/ack field helpers (big-endian u32).

    tcp_write_u32(frame: *byte, off: int, v: u32)
        frame[off]     = byte((v >> 24) & u32(0xff))
        frame[off + 1] = byte((v >> 16) & u32(0xff))
        frame[off + 2] = byte((v >>  8) & u32(0xff))
        frame[off + 3] = byte( v        & u32(0xff))

    tcp_read_u32(frame: *byte, off: int) -> u32
        val b0 = u32(frame[off]     & 0xff)
        val b1 = u32(frame[off + 1] & 0xff)
        val b2 = u32(frame[off + 2] & 0xff)
        val b3 = u32(frame[off + 3] & 0xff)
        (b0 << 24) | (b1 << 16) | (b2 << 8) | b3


Walk TCP options in the segment starting at `tcp_off + 20` and
ending at `tcp_off + hdr_len`. Returns 1 if a SACK-permitted
option (RFC 2018 §2, kind=4 len=2) is present, 0 otherwise.

`kind=0` (EOL) terminates. `kind=1` (NOP) is a single-byte
skip. Everything else has `len` at `off+1`; we bounds-check
to tolerate malformed segments without reading past the
header end. Unknown options are skipped.

    tcp_opts_has_sack_permitted(frame: *byte, tcp_off: int,
                                hdr_len: int) -> int
        var off = tcp_off + 20
        val opts_end = tcp_off + hdr_len
        while off < opts_end
            val kind = int(frame[off]) & 0xff
            if kind == TCP_OPT_EOL
                return 0
            if kind == TCP_OPT_NOP
                off += 1
            else
                if off + 1 >= opts_end
                    return 0
                val olen = int(frame[off + 1]) & 0xff
                if olen < 2
                    return 0
                if off + olen > opts_end
                    return 0
                if kind == TCP_OPT_SACK_PERMITTED
                    if olen == 2
                        return 1
                off += olen
        0


RFC 7323 §2 Window Scale option parser. Returns 1 (with `shift`
filled in, clamped to 0..14 per §2.3) if a WS option is present
in the TCP header, 0 otherwise. Only meaningful on SYN segments
since the option is exchanged exactly once during the handshake.

    tcp_opts_get_wscale(frame: *byte, tcp_off: int, hdr_len: int,
                        out shift: int) -> int
        shift = 0
        var off = tcp_off + 20
        val opts_end = tcp_off + hdr_len
        while off < opts_end
            val kind = int(frame[off]) & 0xff
            if kind == TCP_OPT_EOL
                return 0
            if kind == TCP_OPT_NOP
                off += 1
            else
                if off + 1 >= opts_end
                    return 0
                val olen = int(frame[off + 1]) & 0xff
                if olen < 2
                    return 0
                if off + olen > opts_end
                    return 0
                if kind == TCP_OPT_WSCALE
                    if olen == 3
                        var s = int(frame[off + 2]) & 0xff
                        if s > 14
                            s = 14
                        shift = s
                        return 1
                off += olen
        0


RFC 7323 §3 Timestamps option parser. Returns 1 (with `tsval`
and `tsecr` filled in) if a TS option is present, 0 otherwise.
Caller is responsible for the PAWS check and for echoing
`tsval` back as `tsecr` on the next outbound segment via
`ts_recent`.

    tcp_opts_get_ts(frame: *byte, tcp_off: int, hdr_len: int,
                    out tsval: u32, out tsecr: u32) -> int
        tsval = u32(0)
        tsecr = u32(0)
        var off = tcp_off + 20
        val opts_end = tcp_off + hdr_len
        while off < opts_end
            val kind = int(frame[off]) & 0xff
            if kind == TCP_OPT_EOL
                return 0
            if kind == TCP_OPT_NOP
                off += 1
            else
                if off + 1 >= opts_end
                    return 0
                val olen = int(frame[off + 1]) & 0xff
                if olen < 2
                    return 0
                if off + olen > opts_end
                    return 0
                if kind == TCP_OPT_TS
                    if olen == 10
                        tsval = tcp_read_u32(frame, off + 2)
                        tsecr = tcp_read_u32(frame, off + 6)
                        return 1
                off += olen
        0


Merge a new SACK block `[seq, end)` into socket `s`'s scoreboard.
Coalesces with overlapping or adjacent existing blocks; drops
blocks wholly to the left of `snd_una` (cumulative ACK already
covers them). Caller guarantees `seq < end`.

    tcp_sack_board_insert(s: int, left: u32, right: u32)
        if tcp_seq_le(right, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una) != 0
            return
        var nleft = left
        var nright = right
        if tcp_seq_lt(nleft, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una) != 0
            nleft = tcp_sockets[s].snd_una
        var bi = 0
        while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            if tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use != 0
                val bleft = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].left
                val bright = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].right
                val overlap = tcp_seq_le(nleft, bright) != 0
                if overlap
                    if tcp_seq_le(bleft, nright) != 0
                        if tcp_seq_lt(bleft, nleft) != 0
                            nleft = bleft
                        if tcp_seq_lt(nright, bright) != 0
                            nright = bright
                        tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use = 0
            bi += 1
        bi = 0
        while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            if tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use == 0
                tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use = 1
                tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].left = nleft
                tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].right = nright
                return
            bi += 1


Purge scoreboard entries covered by the advanced `snd_una`.
Blocks strictly to the left get dropped; blocks straddling the
boundary get left-clipped.

    tcp_sack_board_purge(s: int)
        var bi = 0
        while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            if tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use != 0
                val bright = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].right
                if tcp_seq_le(bright, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una) != 0
                    tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use = 0
                else
                    val bleft = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].left
                    if tcp_seq_lt(bleft, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una) != 0
                        tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].left = tcp_sockets[s].snd_una
            bi += 1


Clear all scoreboard entries. Called on full recovery exit and
socket reuse — we don't want a stale block to influence the
next connection or retransmit round.

    tcp_sack_board_clear(s: int)
        var bi = 0
        while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use = 0
            bi += 1
        tcp_sockets[s].sack_rexmit_valid = 0


Parse every `Kind=5` SACK option in the TCP options area and
insert each block into the socket's scoreboard. Unknown,
malformed, or duplicate options are tolerated: the parser
bails early on the first length inconsistency rather than
reading past the header.

    tcp_opts_apply_sack(s: int, frame: *byte, tcp_off: int,
                        hdr_len: int)
        var off = tcp_off + 20
        val opts_end = tcp_off + hdr_len
        while off < opts_end
            val kind = int(frame[off]) & 0xff
            if kind == TCP_OPT_EOL
                return
            if kind == TCP_OPT_NOP
                off += 1
            else
                if off + 1 >= opts_end
                    return
                val olen = int(frame[off + 1]) & 0xff
                if olen < 2
                    return
                if off + olen > opts_end
                    return
                if kind == TCP_OPT_SACK
                    // Block payload: (olen - 2) bytes = 8n,
                    // n ∈ {1..4}. Anything else is malformed.
                    val payload_len = olen - 2
                    if payload_len >= 8
                        if (payload_len % 8) == 0
                            var bo = off + 2
                            val bend_off = off + olen
                            while bo + 8 <= bend_off
                                val blk_left = tcp_read_u32(frame, bo)
                                val blk_right = tcp_read_u32(frame, bo + 4)
                                if tcp_seq_lt(blk_left, blk_right) != 0
                                    tcp_sack_board_insert(s, blk_left, blk_right)
                                bo += 8
                off += olen


Find the next un-SACKed byte `≥ cursor` within `[snd_una, snd_nxt)`,
or `snd_nxt` if no hole remains. Straightforward scan over the
scoreboard: if any block covers `cursor`, jump `cursor` to its
end and restart. Terminates because each jump advances cursor
by ≥ 1 byte and the scoreboard has at most `TCP_SACK_BOARD`
blocks — so at most `TCP_SACK_BOARD + 1` passes.

    tcp_sack_next_hole(s: int, cursor: u32) -> u32
        var c = cursor
        if tcp_seq_lt(c, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una) != 0
            c = tcp_sockets[s].snd_una
        var passes = 0
        while passes <= TCP_SACK_BOARD
            var moved = 0
            var bi = 0
            while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
                if tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].in_use != 0
                    val bleft = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].left
                    val bright = tcp_sockets[s].sack_board[bi].right
                    if tcp_seq_le(bleft, c) != 0
                        if tcp_seq_lt(c, bright) != 0
                            c = bright
                            moved = 1
                bi += 1
            if moved == 0
                return c
            passes += 1
        c


PMTU-driven effective MSS (RFC 1191 §5). The send-side payload
cap is `pmtu - IP(20) - TCP(<=40 with options)`. Slix's drain
path picks the segment size before knowing the exact option
shape — Timestamps + SACK can grow the header to 36 or 40
bytes — so we conservatively reserve 40 bytes for the TCP
header. Over-conservative just means slightly smaller
segments on the wire, never a frame-too-big rejection inside
`inet_tcp_emit`. Loopback destinations hit the same lookup;
the default-MTU miss returns 1500 so the existing TCP_MAX_SEG
ceiling continues to dominate when the cache hasn't learned
anything.

A floor of 1 byte prevents a degenerate cap from stalling the
connection — if a peer reports an MTU below the architectural
minimum the cache clamps it to `INET_PMTU_MIN=296` anyway, so
this floor is belt-and-suspenders.

    inet_tcp_eff_mss(s: int) -> int
        val pmtu = inet_pmtu_get(tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip)
        var cap = pmtu - 20 - 40
        if cap < 1
            cap = 1
        cap

`inet_tcp_data_seg_emit_count` ticks whenever `inet_tcp_emit`
sends a segment carrying user payload (any non-zero
`payload_len`). Pure ACKs, retransmits with payload, SYN/FIN
without data, and the loopback fastpath all pass through —
the counter records data delivery as observed at the emit
seam, not the wire. Test scaffolding reads this counter
across a `send()` to assert the segment count matches what a
PMTU-clamped path would produce.

    var inet_tcp_data_seg_emit_count: int = 0


Build a single Ethernet + IPv4 + TCP frame for socket `s`. Payload
is `payload_len` bytes of send_buf starting at `payload_seq`
(relative to snd_una).

TCP options attached:

- Active SYN frames advertise MSS + SACK-permitted + Timestamps
  + Window-scale: 20 bytes of options, `hdr_bytes = 40`.
- SYN-ACK frames echo TS / WS only if the peer's SYN advertised
  them.  The exact layout follows from the include flags below.
- Post-handshake segments include a 12-byte TS block (2 NOPs +
  10-byte option) once TS has been negotiated, plus an optional
  SACK block when reorder slots are populated.
- All other segments carry no options — `hdr_bytes = 20`.

    inet_tcp_emit(s: int, flags: int, seq: u32,
                  payload_seq: u32, payload_len: int) -> int
        // Test-only ACK blackhole. When `ack_blackholed != 0`, drop
        // pure-ACK segments (ACK set, no payload, no SYN/FIN/RST).
        // SYN/FIN/RST still fly so the test can establish + tear
        // down the connection; only acknowledgements are silently
        // discarded, which on slix's synchronous loopback fastpath
        // is the only way to simulate "peer went dark" without
        // breaking handshake.
        if tcp_sockets[s].ack_blackholed != 0
            val pure_ack_mask = TCP_F_ACK
            val deny_mask = TCP_F_SYN | TCP_F_FIN | TCP_F_RST
            if (flags & deny_mask) == 0
                if (flags & pure_ack_mask) != 0
                    if payload_len == 0
                        return 0
        if inet_our_mac_known == 0
            return -1
        val is_loopback = inet_is_loopback_ip(tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip)
        var next_hop_mac: [6]byte
        if is_loopback != 0
            // Loopback never goes to the NIC; the eth header is
            // ignored on the inbound dispatch path so any MAC is
            // fine. Re-using `inet_our_mac` keeps construction
            // uniform with the wire path below.
            var mi = 0
            while mi < 6
                next_hop_mac[mi] = inet_our_mac[mi]
                mi += 1
        else
            if inet_resolve_mac(tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip, next_hop_mac) == 0
                // ARP request now in-flight.  Mark the slot so
                // `inet_arp_drain_pending` can re-emit when the
                // reply lands, instead of waiting an RTO.
                tcp_sockets[s].arp_pending = 1
                return -1
        val is_syn = (flags & TCP_F_SYN) != 0
        // Count SACK blocks we'd emit on this segment. SACK is
        // only ever attached to non-SYN ACKs once both sides
        // agreed on SACK-permitted at handshake.
        var sack_count = 0
        if is_syn == 0
            if (flags & TCP_F_ACK) != 0
                if tcp_sockets[s].sack_ok != 0
                    var ri = 0
                    while ri < TCP_REORDER_SLOTS
                        if tcp_sockets[s].reorder[ri].in_use != 0
                            if sack_count < TCP_MAX_SACK_BLOCKS
                                sack_count += 1
                        ri += 1
        // RFC 7323 negotiate-and-echo rules. Active SYN (no ACK
        // bit) advertises everything we support. SYN-ACK only
        // includes options the peer's SYN already opted into.
        // Post-handshake includes TS on every segment iff TS was
        // negotiated.
        var include_ts = 0
        var include_ws = 0
        if is_syn
            if (flags & TCP_F_ACK) == 0
                include_ts = 1
                include_ws = 1
            else
                if tcp_sockets[s].ts_ok != 0
                    include_ts = 1
                if tcp_sockets[s].rcv_wscale != 0
                    include_ws = 1
        else
            if tcp_sockets[s].ts_ok != 0
                include_ts = 1
        var hdr_bytes = 20
        if is_syn
            // MSS(4) + SACK_PERM(2) = 6, then variable trailers
            // chosen so the total stays 4-byte aligned.
            if include_ts != 0 && include_ws != 0
                hdr_bytes = 40
            else if include_ts != 0
                hdr_bytes = 36
            else if include_ws != 0
                hdr_bytes = 32
            else
                hdr_bytes = 28
        else
            if include_ts != 0
                hdr_bytes = 32
                if sack_count > 0
                    hdr_bytes = 32 + 4 + 8 * sack_count
            else if sack_count > 0
                hdr_bytes = 20 + 4 + 8 * sack_count
        var seg_len = hdr_bytes + payload_len
        if seg_len > 1460
            return -1
        if payload_len > 0
            inet_tcp_data_seg_emit_count += 1
        val fidx = inet_frame_alloc()
        val frame = inet_frame_buf(fidx)
        val total_len = 14 + 20 + seg_len
        pkt_write_mac(frame, 0, next_hop_mac)
        pkt_write_mac(frame, 6, inet_our_mac)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 12, ETHERTYPE_IPV4)
        frame[14] = 0x45
        frame[15] = 0
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 16, 20 + seg_len)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 18, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 20, 0x4000)
        frame[22] = 64
        frame[23] = byte(IP_PROTO_TCP)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 24, 0)
        val our_ip = ipv4(INET_OUR_IP_A, INET_OUR_IP_B,
            INET_OUR_IP_C, INET_OUR_IP_D)
        pkt_write_ipv4(frame, 26,
            INET_OUR_IP_A, INET_OUR_IP_B, INET_OUR_IP_C, INET_OUR_IP_D)
        pkt_write_ipv4(frame, 30,
            ipv4_octet(tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip, 0),
            ipv4_octet(tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip, 1),
            ipv4_octet(tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip, 2),
            ipv4_octet(tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip, 3))
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 24, net_checksum(frame + 14, 20))
        val tcp_off = 34
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 0, tcp_sockets[s].local_port)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 2, tcp_sockets[s].remote_port)
        tcp_write_u32(frame, tcp_off + 4, seq)
        var ack_val: u32 = u32(0)
        if (flags & TCP_F_ACK) != 0
            ack_val = tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt
        tcp_write_u32(frame, tcp_off + 8, ack_val)
        val data_off_words = hdr_bytes / 4
        frame[tcp_off + 12] = byte((data_off_words << 4) & 0xf0)
        frame[tcp_off + 13] = byte(flags & 0xff)
        val stashed = inet_tcp_reorder_bytes(s)
        var rwnd = tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size - tcp_sockets[s].recv_len - stashed
        if rwnd < 0
            rwnd = 0
        // RFC 7323 §2.2: SYN segments advertise the unscaled
        // window field; non-SYN segments advertise (rwnd >>
        // rcv_wscale). When `rcv_wscale == 0` (no negotiation)
        // the shift is a no-op.
        var rwnd_field = rwnd
        if is_syn == 0
            rwnd_field = rwnd >> tcp_sockets[s].rcv_wscale
        if rwnd_field > 0xffff
            rwnd_field = 0xffff
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 14, rwnd_field)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 16, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 18, 0)
        // Compute the values we'll write into the TS option.
        // tsval baked from a per-connection random offset so
        // the host kernel's tick count doesn't leak; tsecr
        // mirrors `ts_recent`, which is 0 on the initial active
        // SYN (peer hasn't advertised yet).
        val tsval = u32(monotonic_ms()) + tcp_sockets[s].ts_offset
        val tsecr = tcp_sockets[s].ts_recent
        if is_syn
            // MSS (RFC 793): kind=2 len=4 value=TCP_MSS_ADV
            frame[tcp_off + 20] = byte(TCP_OPT_MSS)
            frame[tcp_off + 21] = 4
            pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 22, TCP_MSS_ADV)
            // SACK-permitted (RFC 2018 §2): kind=4 len=2
            frame[tcp_off + 24] = byte(TCP_OPT_SACK_PERMITTED)
            frame[tcp_off + 25] = 2
            if include_ts != 0 && include_ws != 0
                // TS at +26..35, NOP at +36, WS at +37..39.
                frame[tcp_off + 26] = byte(TCP_OPT_TS)
                frame[tcp_off + 27] = 10
                tcp_write_u32(frame, tcp_off + 28, tsval)
                tcp_write_u32(frame, tcp_off + 32, tsecr)
                frame[tcp_off + 36] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
                frame[tcp_off + 37] = byte(TCP_OPT_WSCALE)
                frame[tcp_off + 38] = 3
                frame[tcp_off + 39] = byte(TCP_RCV_WSCALE)
            else if include_ts != 0
                // TS at +26..35, already aligned.
                frame[tcp_off + 26] = byte(TCP_OPT_TS)
                frame[tcp_off + 27] = 10
                tcp_write_u32(frame, tcp_off + 28, tsval)
                tcp_write_u32(frame, tcp_off + 32, tsecr)
            else if include_ws != 0
                // 2 NOPs at +26..27, WS at +28..30, NOP at +31.
                frame[tcp_off + 26] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
                frame[tcp_off + 27] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
                frame[tcp_off + 28] = byte(TCP_OPT_WSCALE)
                frame[tcp_off + 29] = 3
                frame[tcp_off + 30] = byte(TCP_RCV_WSCALE)
                frame[tcp_off + 31] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
            else
                // Pad to 4-byte alignment with NOPs (legacy layout).
                frame[tcp_off + 26] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
                frame[tcp_off + 27] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
        else
            var opts_cur = tcp_off + 20
            if include_ts != 0
                // 2 NOPs + TS option = 12 bytes, naturally aligned.
                frame[opts_cur] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
                frame[opts_cur + 1] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
                frame[opts_cur + 2] = byte(TCP_OPT_TS)
                frame[opts_cur + 3] = 10
                tcp_write_u32(frame, opts_cur + 4, tsval)
                tcp_write_u32(frame, opts_cur + 8, tsecr)
                opts_cur += 12
            if sack_count > 0
                // 2 NOPs prefix for alignment (Linux emits this
                // pattern too) — placing SACK at a 4-byte boundary.
                frame[opts_cur]     = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
                frame[opts_cur + 1] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
                // SACK header: kind=5, len = 2 + 8 * nblocks.
                frame[opts_cur + 2] = byte(TCP_OPT_SACK)
                frame[opts_cur + 3] = byte(2 + 8 * sack_count)
                var out_off = opts_cur + 4
                var ri2 = 0
                var emitted = 0
                while ri2 < TCP_REORDER_SLOTS
                    if emitted < sack_count
                        if tcp_sockets[s].reorder[ri2].in_use != 0
                            val blk_left = tcp_sockets[s].reorder[ri2].seq
                            val blk_right = blk_left + u32(tcp_sockets[s].reorder[ri2].len)
                            tcp_write_u32(frame, out_off, blk_left)
                            tcp_write_u32(frame, out_off + 4, blk_right)
                            out_off += 8
                            emitted += 1
                    ri2 += 1
        if payload_len > 0
            val offset = int(i32(payload_seq - tcp_sockets[s].snd_una))
            var i = 0
            while i < payload_len
                frame[tcp_off + hdr_bytes + i] = tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_ptr[offset + i]
                i += 1
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 16,
            (~inet_l4_partial_sum(our_ip, tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip,
                IP_PROTO_TCP, frame, tcp_off, seg_len)) & 0xffff)
        if is_loopback != 0
            // Swap IP src/dst so the inbound dispatcher sees a
            // peer-shaped frame, then recompute IP and TCP
            // checksums against the new pseudo-header. The TCP
            // pseudo-header was originally `(our_ip, remote_ip)`;
            // on inbound it will be evaluated as `(src_from_frame,
            // our_ip)`, so swapping puts both into agreement.
            var sw = 0
            while sw < 4
                val tmp = frame[26 + sw]
                frame[26 + sw] = frame[30 + sw]
                frame[30 + sw] = tmp
                sw += 1
            pkt_write_u16(frame, 24, 0)
            pkt_write_u16(frame, 24, net_checksum(frame + 14, 20))
            val src_in = ipv4(int(frame[26]) & 0xff, int(frame[27]) & 0xff,
                int(frame[28]) & 0xff, int(frame[29]) & 0xff)
            val dst_in = ipv4(int(frame[30]) & 0xff, int(frame[31]) & 0xff,
                int(frame[32]) & 0xff, int(frame[33]) & 0xff)
            pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 16, 0)
            pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 16,
                (~inet_l4_partial_sum(src_in, dst_in,
                    IP_PROTO_TCP, frame, tcp_off, seg_len)) & 0xffff)
            inet_tcp_handle_segment(frame, total_len, 20)
            inet_frame_release(fidx)
            return 0
        inet_nic_send_packet(frame, total_len)
        inet_frame_release(fidx)
        0


    inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s: int)
        tcp_sockets[s].rtx_deadline = int(monotonic_ms()) + tcp_sockets[s].rto

    inet_tcp_disarm_rtx(s: int)
        tcp_sockets[s].rtx_deadline = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].retries = 0


RFC 6298 RTT measurement + RTO update. Caller guarantees `r` is
ticks (clock granularity = 1 tick).

First measurement: SRTT = R, RTTVAR = R/2, RTO = SRTT + 4*RTTVAR.
Subsequent:        RTTVAR += (|SRTT - R| - RTTVAR) >> 2
                   SRTT   += (R - SRTT) >> 3
                   RTO    =  SRTT + 4*RTTVAR

RTO is clamped to [TCP_RTO_MS_MIN, TCP_RTO_MS_MAX].

    inet_tcp_rtt_update(s: int, r: int)
        var rr = r
        if rr < 1
            rr = 1
        if tcp_sockets[s].rtt_measured == 0
            tcp_sockets[s].srtt = rr
            tcp_sockets[s].rttvar = rr >> 1
            tcp_sockets[s].rtt_measured = 1
        else
            var diff = tcp_sockets[s].srtt - rr
            if diff < 0
                diff = 0 - diff
            val old_var = tcp_sockets[s].rttvar
            val old_srtt = tcp_sockets[s].srtt
            tcp_sockets[s].rttvar = (3 * old_var + diff) >> 2
            tcp_sockets[s].srtt = (7 * old_srtt + rr) >> 3
            if tcp_sockets[s].srtt < 1
                tcp_sockets[s].srtt = 1
        var new_rto = tcp_sockets[s].srtt + (tcp_sockets[s].rttvar << 2)
        if new_rto < TCP_RTO_MS_MIN
            new_rto = TCP_RTO_MS_MIN
        if new_rto > TCP_RTO_MS_MAX
            new_rto = TCP_RTO_MS_MAX
        tcp_sockets[s].rto = new_rto

Karn's rule: after a retransmit the original segment's timing is
ambiguous, so discard any in-flight RTT sample. Doubles the RTO
with cap.

    inet_tcp_rto_backoff(s: int)
        tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_valid = 0
        var doubled = tcp_sockets[s].rto << 1
        if doubled > TCP_RTO_MS_MAX
            doubled = TCP_RTO_MS_MAX
        tcp_sockets[s].rto = doubled

Start an RTT sample for the next ACK covering `expected_ack`. If a
sample is already in flight, keep it — measuring once per RTT is
sufficient per RFC 6298.

    inet_tcp_rtt_start_sample(s: int, expected_ack: u32)
        if tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_valid != 0
            return
        tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_valid = 1
        tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_seq = expected_ack
        tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_time = int(monotonic_ms())

If the incoming ACK covers our sample seq (ack >= sample_seq),
take the RTT measurement and clear the sample.

    inet_tcp_rtt_maybe_sample(s: int, ack: u32)
        if tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_valid == 0
            return
        val target = tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_seq
        if tcp_seq_lt(ack, target) != 0
            return
        val r = int(monotonic_ms()) - tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_time
        tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_valid = 0
        inet_tcp_rtt_update(s, r)


One-byte status reply — close() completion.

    inet_tcp_reply_status(tid: int, status: int)
        var wr: [1]byte
        wr[0] = byte(status & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(tid, wr, 1)


Two-byte connect() reply: status + fd. fd is meaningless on
error but the client still reads it (stable wire shape).

    inet_tcp_reply_connect(tid: int, status: int, fd: int)
        var wr: [2]byte
        wr[0] = byte(status & 0xff)
        wr[1] = byte(fd & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(tid, wr, 2)

Parked SEND reply.  Phase 1 of the net-stack quality plan adds
real parking: when `inet_handle_tcp_send` finds the send buffer
full, it stashes the caller in `send_waiter` instead of replying
with `n=0`, and the ACK-handling path here (via
`inet_tcp_drain_send_waiter`) replies once space frees.  The
reply is `(status=0, written=0)` — pure wake signal.  The shim
loops and the next TCP_SEND now finds room.

    inet_tcp_reply_send(tid: int, status: int, written: int)
        var wr: [3]byte
        wr[0] = byte(status & 0xff)
        pkt_write_u16(wr, 1, written)
        ipc_reply(tid, wr, 3)


Wake the parked send_waiter on send-buf drain.  Called from the
ACK-handling path in `inet_tcp_state_step` after `send_len`
shrinks: if a sender is parked and there is now any free space,
reply `(0, 0)` so the shim retries the same chunk against the
freshly-opened window.

    inet_tcp_drain_send_waiter(s: int)
        if tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter >= 0
            val space = tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size - tcp_sockets[s].send_len
            if space > 0
                val w = tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter
                tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter = -1
                tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter_len = 0
                tcp_sockets[s].send_deadline = 0
                inet_tcp_reply_send(w, 0, 0)

RECV drain + reply. `n == 0` signals peer-closed EOF.

    inet_tcp_reply_recv(s: int, n: int)
        val tid = tcp_sockets[s].recv_waiter
        tcp_sockets[s].recv_waiter = -1
        var wr: [540]byte
        wr[0] = 0
        pkt_write_u16(wr, 1, n)
        var i = 0
        while i < n
            wr[3 + i] = tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr[i]
            i += 1
        if n > 0
            var j = n
            while j < tcp_sockets[s].recv_len
                tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr[j - n] = tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr[j]
                j += 1
            tcp_sockets[s].recv_len -= n
        ipc_reply(tid, wr, 3 + n)


Free socket `s` and wake any parked waiter with `status`. Used on
terminal ACK and on retransmit exhaustion.

Enter TIME_WAIT for 2MSL. Unblocks any parked close() caller
immediately (the handshake is complete from the app's point of
view) but leaves the slot live until tw_deadline fires — so the
stack can absorb a retransmitted peer FIN and stale duplicates.

    inet_tcp_enter_time_wait(s: int)
        if tcp_sockets[s].close_waiter >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_status(tcp_sockets[s].close_waiter, 0)
            tcp_sockets[s].close_waiter = -1
        tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_TIME_WAIT
        tcp_sockets[s].rtx_deadline = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].tw_deadline = int(monotonic_ms()) + TCP_TW_MS


    inet_tcp_close_slot(s: int, status: int)
        // Fire epoll readiness *before* tearing the slot down so any
        // EPOLL_CTL_DEL on the slot still reaches the right sub list.
        // The shim re-queries via INET_CMD_POLL on wake; once the slot
        // is in_use=0 the poll returns mask=0 and the shim simply
        // reports nothing — preferring the wasted wake over an extra
        // pre-tear-down readiness state.
        inet_epoll_fire(2, s)
        // Phase 3 chunk 3: drop from the connected-socket hash. No-op
        // for slots that never carried a 4-tuple (LISTEN slots, fresh
        // alloc that was immediately close_slot'd) — the bucket walk
        // simply finds nothing and returns.
        inet_tcp_conn_hash_remove(s)
        // Phase 3 chunk 4: drop from the listen hash if this was a
        // listener. Gated on `is_listen` because non-listener slots
        // were never inserted; the gate avoids the bucket walk.
        if tcp_sockets[s].is_listen != 0
            inet_tcp_listen_hash_remove(s)
        if tcp_sockets[s].connect_waiter >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_connect(tcp_sockets[s].connect_waiter, status, 0)
            tcp_sockets[s].connect_waiter = -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].recv_waiter >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(s, 0)
        if tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_send(tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter, status, 0)
            tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter = -1
            tcp_sockets[s].send_deadline = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].user_timeout_ms = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].earliest_unack_ms = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].ack_blackholed = 0
        if tcp_sockets[s].close_waiter >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_status(tcp_sockets[s].close_waiter, 0)
            tcp_sockets[s].close_waiter = -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].accept_waiter >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_accept(tcp_sockets[s].accept_waiter, status, 0)
            tcp_sockets[s].accept_waiter = -1
        tcp_sockets[s].in_use = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_CLOSED
        // owners ring intentionally NOT cleared here — `owners[0]`
        // survives close to gate post-close SO_ERROR reads via
        // `inet_tcp_owners_was_last`. `inet_tcp_alloc` calls
        // `inet_tcp_owners_init` to reset on slot reuse.
        tcp_sockets[s].is_listen = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].accept_count = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].accept_head = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].accept_tail = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].accept_backlog = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].parent_listen = -1
        tcp_sockets[s].rtx_deadline = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].tw_deadline = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].fin_pending = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].srtt = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].rttvar = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].rto = TCP_RTO_MS_INITIAL
        tcp_sockets[s].rtt_measured = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_valid = 0
        var k = 0
        while k < TCP_REORDER_SLOTS
            tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].in_use = 0
            k += 1
        tcp_sack_board_clear(s)
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_wscale = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].rcv_wscale = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].ts_ok = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].ts_recent = u32(0)
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_on = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].last_recv_ms = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_probes = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_next_probe = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_idle = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_intvl = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_cnt = 0
        // Release the heap-backed buffers. `free(null)` is a no-op
        // (std.alloc), so close paths hit on slots that never
        // allocated (e.g. listen sockets in early-reject paths) are
        // safe.  Null the pointers + zero the sizes so a fresh
        // alloc starts from a clean slate.
        free(tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_ptr)
        free(tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr)
        tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_ptr = *byte(0)
        tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr = *byte(0)
        tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size = 0


SO_LINGER `l_linger=0` close path. Emit RST + close the slot,
skipping FIN/TIME_WAIT entirely (Linux's "abort close" pattern —
the application is done with the connection and wants the peer
to see RST so a half-broken transfer fails fast).

Caller is responsible for owner_tid validation.  Returns 0 on
success, -1 when the slot is already closed (idempotent).

    inet_tcp_abort(s: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        // Only emit RST when the connection has progressed past
        // active SYN — a slot that never reached SYN_RCVD has no
        // peer mac to send to.  For SYN_SENT we still RST so the
        // peer's listening socket clears its half-open state.
        val st = tcp_sockets[s].state
        if st != TCP_STATE_CLOSED
            if st != TCP_STATE_LISTEN
                inet_tcp_emit(s,
                    TCP_F_RST | TCP_F_ACK,
                    tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt,
                    u32(0), 0)
        inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
        0


    inet_tcp_alloc() -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            if tcp_sockets[i].in_use == 0
                tcp_sockets[i].in_use = 1
                tcp_sockets[i].state = TCP_STATE_CLOSED
                inet_tcp_owners_init(i)
                tcp_sockets[i].queued_refs = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].send_len = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].recv_len = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].retries = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].rtx_deadline = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].tw_deadline = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].peer_fin = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].our_fin_sent = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].connect_waiter = -1
                tcp_sockets[i].send_waiter = -1
                tcp_sockets[i].send_deadline = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].user_timeout_ms = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].earliest_unack_ms = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].ack_blackholed = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].recv_waiter = -1
                tcp_sockets[i].close_waiter = -1
                tcp_sockets[i].mss = TCP_MSS_ADV
                tcp_sockets[i].is_listen = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].accept_head = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].accept_tail = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].accept_count = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].accept_backlog = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].accept_waiter = -1
                tcp_sockets[i].parent_listen = -1
                tcp_sockets[i].fin_pending = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].srtt = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].rttvar = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].rto = TCP_RTO_MS_INITIAL
                tcp_sockets[i].rtt_measured = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].rtt_sample_valid = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].snd_cwnd = TCP_INIT_CWND
                tcp_sockets[i].snd_ssthresh = TCP_INIT_SSTHRESH
                tcp_sockets[i].dup_ack_count = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].fast_recovery = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].recovery_point = u32(0)
                tcp_sockets[i].sack_ok = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].sack_rexmit_valid = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].rexmit_count = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].fire_seq = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].snd_wscale = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].rcv_wscale = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].ts_ok = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].ts_recent = u32(0)
                // Mix slot index, alloc seq, and monotonic_ms so two
                // back-to-back connections from the same socket
                // pool don't share an offset.
                tcp_sockets[i].ts_offset = u32(monotonic_ms()) ^ u32(i * 1000003) ^ tcp_iss_counter
                tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_on = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].last_recv_ms = int(monotonic_ms())
                tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_probes = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_next_probe = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_idle = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_intvl = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_cnt = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].pending_error = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].arp_pending = 0
                tcp_sockets[i].next_in_tcp_conn_bucket = -1
                tcp_sockets[i].next_in_tcp_listen_bucket = -1
                // Heap-backed buffers are allocated lazily on alloc.
                // close_slot frees + nulls the pointers, so re-using a
                // slot doesn't double-allocate.  std.alloc returns 0 on
                // OOM; we accept the leak rather than fail open since
                // a fresh boot won't exhaust the inet heap and the
                // soft-failure path would race the rest of the init.
                tcp_sockets[i].send_buf_ptr = malloc(i64(TCP_SEND_BUF_DEFAULT))
                tcp_sockets[i].send_buf_size = TCP_SEND_BUF_DEFAULT
                tcp_sockets[i].recv_buf_ptr = malloc(i64(TCP_RECV_BUF_DEFAULT))
                tcp_sockets[i].recv_buf_size = TCP_RECV_BUF_DEFAULT
                var k = 0
                while k < TCP_REORDER_SLOTS
                    tcp_sockets[i].reorder[k].in_use = 0
                    k += 1
                var bi = 0
                while bi < TCP_SACK_BOARD
                    tcp_sockets[i].sack_board[bi].in_use = 0
                    bi += 1
                return i
            i += 1
        -1


setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF) / setsockopt(SO_RCVBUF) implementation. Resizes
the heap-backed send or recv buffer on socket `s`. `kind` selects
which buffer (0 = SNDBUF, 1 = RCVBUF). `size` is the requested new
size in bytes (clamped to [TCP_BUF_MIN, TCP_BUF_MAX]).

Resize only happens in the CLOSED state with no buffered data. After
connect/listen/accept, Linux silently accepts the call but ignores
the new value (`tcp_set_rcvbuf` warns at most once); we mirror that
"return 0, no-op" behavior so well-behaved servers that call
`setsockopt` after `accept` don't get spurious EINVALs. Returns 0 on
success (whether or not a resize actually happened), -1 on bad fd or
out-of-range kind.

    inet_tcp_resize_buf(s: int, kind: int, size: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if kind != 0 && kind != 1
            return -1
        var clamped = size
        if clamped < TCP_BUF_MIN
            clamped = TCP_BUF_MIN
        if clamped > TCP_BUF_MAX
            clamped = TCP_BUF_MAX
        // Phase 2 chunk 2 — per-tid TCP buffer-memory cap.  Linux-
        // ish silent clamp: if growing the buffer would push owner
        // over TCP_PER_TID_BUF_MAX, shrink the request to whatever
        // budget remains. owners_count == 0 means alloc has run but
        // begin_*/handle_passive_syn hasn't stamped yet — skip the
        // check; the caller will own the budget after stamping.
        val owner = inet_tcp_owners_primary(s)
        if owner >= 0
            var current = tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size
            if kind != 0
                current = tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size
            if clamped > current
                val others = inet_tcp_buf_bytes_owner(owner) - current
                val budget = TCP_PER_TID_BUF_MAX - others
                if clamped > budget
                    clamped = budget
                if clamped < TCP_BUF_MIN
                    return 0
        // Only the CLOSED state with empty buffers is a safe resize
        // window: connect/listen latch the buffer pointer into flow-
        // control state, and copying live in-flight data on a
        // realloc would race the receive path.
        if tcp_sockets[s].state != TCP_STATE_CLOSED
            return 0
        if kind == 0
            if tcp_sockets[s].send_len != 0
                return 0
            if clamped == tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size
                return 0
            free(tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_ptr)
            tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_ptr = malloc(i64(clamped))
            tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size = clamped
        else
            if tcp_sockets[s].recv_len != 0
                return 0
            if clamped == tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size
                return 0
            free(tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr)
            tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr = malloc(i64(clamped))
            tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size = clamped
        0


setsockopt(SO_KEEPALIVE) — flips the per-socket keepalive flag.
Turning it on baselines `last_recv_ms` to now and clears any
in-flight probe count, so a freshly-enabled socket waits a full
`TCP_KEEPALIVE_IDLE_MS` from this point before the first probe.
Turning it off zeroes the schedule.

Idempotent (no-op for same-state calls).  Returns 0 on success,
-1 on bad fd / closed slot.

    inet_tcp_set_keepalive(s: int, on: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if on != 0
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_on = 1
            tcp_sockets[s].last_recv_ms = int(monotonic_ms())
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_probes = 0
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_next_probe = 0
        else
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_on = 0
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_probes = 0
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_next_probe = 0
        0


setsockopt(TCP_USER_TIMEOUT) — write the per-socket cap in
milliseconds (the unit the user already passed at the POSIX
boundary; RFC 5482 §3 is in ms, the man page is misleading).
0 disables (Linux's "system default" semantics — slix has no
sysctl for this, so 0 means "no cap").

Returns 0 on success, -1 on bad fd.

    inet_tcp_set_userto(s: int, timeout_ms: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        var t = timeout_ms
        if t < 0
            t = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].user_timeout_ms = t
        0


Test-only: arm or disarm the ACK blackhole on a slot.  When set,
`inet_tcp_emit` drops pure-ACK segments from this slot, so the
peer never sees acknowledgements while SYN/FIN/RST handshake
flow continues.  Used by `slix/test/musrto.c` to drive a
TCP_USER_TIMEOUT abort over the loopback fastpath, which would
otherwise ACK every segment synchronously.

Returns 0 on success, -1 on bad fd.

    inet_tcp_set_ack_blackhole(s: int, on: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if on != 0
            tcp_sockets[s].ack_blackholed = 1
        else
            tcp_sockets[s].ack_blackholed = 0
        0


setsockopt(TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT) — write a
per-socket override that scan_timers consults instead of the
global default. `kind` selects which option (0 = idle, 1 = intvl,
2 = cnt). Idle/intvl arrive in *milliseconds* (the shim has already
converted from seconds via `secs * 1000`); cnt is a raw probe count.
Clamp to a non-zero positive — 0 is reserved internally to mean
"use default", so a caller passing 0 still routes to the global
constant. Resets the probe schedule so the new cadence takes
effect on the next scan, regardless of any in-flight probe.

Returns 0 on success, -1 on bad fd / unknown kind.

    inet_tcp_set_keepopt(s: int, kind: int, value: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        var v = value
        if v < 0
            v = 0
        if kind == 0
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_idle = v
        else if kind == 1
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_intvl = v
        else if kind == 2
            tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_cnt = v
        else
            return -1
        // Force a reschedule on the next scan: any cached probe
        // deadline was derived from the old cadence and would
        // misbehave (probe too soon / too late) under the new
        // values.
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_next_probe = 0
        0


getsockopt(TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT) — read the
persisted per-socket override. Returns the *milliseconds* / count.
The caller (typically the shim) is responsible for converting back
to seconds. Returns -1 on bad fd / unknown kind. A 0 reply means
"using the global default" — distinct from a real 0, which is
forbidden by the setter clamp.

    inet_tcp_get_keepopt(s: int, kind: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if kind == 0
            return tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_idle
        if kind == 1
            return tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_intvl
        if kind == 2
            return tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_cnt
        -1


Resolve the effective keepalive timing for socket `s`. Returns
the values scan_timers should use, falling back to the global
default whenever a per-socket override is 0. Centralized so the
fallback rule stays in one place rather than duplicated three
times in scan_timers.

    inet_tcp_eff_keepalive(s: int, out idle: int, out intvl: int, out cnt: int)
        val raw_idle = tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_idle
        val raw_intvl = tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_intvl
        val raw_cnt = tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_cnt
        idle = if raw_idle != 0 then raw_idle else TCP_KEEPALIVE_IDLE_MS
        intvl = if raw_intvl != 0 then raw_intvl else TCP_KEEPALIVE_INTVL_MS
        cnt = if raw_cnt != 0 then raw_cnt else TCP_KEEPALIVE_MAX_PROBES


    inet_tcp_get_bufsize(s: int, kind: int) -> int
        if s < 0 || s >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[s].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if kind == 0
            return tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size
        if kind == 1
            return tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size
        -1

    inet_tcp_find(local_port: int, remote_ip: u32, remote_port: int) -> int
        var i = inet_tcp_conn_hash[inet_tcp_conn_bucket(local_port, remote_port, remote_ip)]
        while i >= 0
            if (tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0 && tcp_sockets[i].is_listen == 0 &&
                    tcp_sockets[i].local_port == local_port &&
                    tcp_sockets[i].remote_port == remote_port &&
                    tcp_sockets[i].remote_ip == remote_ip)
                return i
            i = tcp_sockets[i].next_in_tcp_conn_bucket
        -1


Find a listen socket bound to `local_port`. Returns slot index or -1.

    inet_tcp_find_listen(local_port: int) -> int
        var i = inet_tcp_listen_hash[inet_tcp_listen_bucket(local_port)]
        while i >= 0
            if (tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0 && tcp_sockets[i].is_listen != 0 &&
                    tcp_sockets[i].local_port == local_port)
                return i
            i = tcp_sockets[i].next_in_tcp_listen_bucket
        -1


Returns the slot index of any in-use TCP socket currently bound to
`local_port` and in a post-handshake state (i.e., a *connected* slot
in ESTABLISHED / FIN_WAIT_* / CLOSE_WAIT / LAST_ACK / CLOSING /
TIME_WAIT, or a SYN_SENT outbound that bound the local port before
calling connect). Used by `inet_tcp_begin_listen` to honor RFC 793 /
1122 §4.2.2.10 — `bind()` (rolled into our `begin_listen`) must
refuse a port still serving the 2MSL TIME_WAIT for a prior
connection (or any other in-flight teardown) unless `SO_REUSEADDR`
was set. Linear scan over `TCP_MAX_SOCKETS` is fine — n = 32 today
and these slots are short-lived; the bucket walk a hash would buy
is overkill. If `tcp_sockets[]` ever grows past a few hundred, fold
into the existing 4-tuple connected-socket hash keyed on
(local_port, *).

`is_listen != 0` is **excluded** because the existing
`inet_tcp_find_listen` already covers that case via its dedicated
hash; SYN_RCVD children of a still-live listener are excluded for
the same reason. Children inherit the listener's port, so blocking
SYN_RCVD here would refuse a re-listen against the same port even
when the listener is being torn down — `do_close LISTEN`'s child-
sweep handles that case directly.

    inet_tcp_find_port_post_listen(local_port: int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if tcp_sockets[i].is_listen == 0
                    val st = tcp_sockets[i].state
                    if st != TCP_STATE_CLOSED && st != TCP_STATE_SYN_RCVD
                        if tcp_sockets[i].local_port == local_port
                            return i
            i += 1
        -1


TCP-private ephemeral port allocator (49152+). Distinct from UDP's
so a TCP connect and a UDP socket can both hold 49152.

    tcp_alloc_port() -> int
        var port = 49152
        while port < 65536
            var taken = 0
            var i = 0
            while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
                if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                    if tcp_sockets[i].local_port == port
                        taken = 1
                        i = TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
                    else
                        i += 1
                else
                    i += 1
            if taken == 0
                return port
            port += 1
        -1


connect() entry — allocate slot, emit SYN, park caller. Returns
the socket index on success (caller blocks until ESTABLISHED),
-1 on immediate failure.

    inet_tcp_begin_connect(owner_tid: int, caller_tid: int,
                           remote_ip: u32, remote_port: int,
                           sndbuf_override: int, rcvbuf_override: int) -> int
        if inet_our_mac_known == 0
            return -1
        // Phase 2 quality plan: per-tid cap on TCP slots.  Literal
        // 8 — duplicated in `inet_handle_socket` for UDP slots
        // (sysl analyzer bug #19: cross-file val and cross-file fn
        // refs break analysis).  The two pools are checked
        // independently, so a tid can hold up to 8 UDP + 8 TCP =
        // 16 slots; with 32 + 16 = 48 total slots that still
        // guarantees at least three concurrent processes.
        if inet_count_tcp_owner(owner_tid) >= 8
            return -1
        // Phase 2 chunk 2: per-tid TCP buffer-memory cap. Refuse
        // up front if the default-sized buffers wouldn't fit
        // under TCP_PER_TID_BUF_MAX. resize_buf later clamps
        // grow attempts but cannot reject the alloc itself.
        val def_total = TCP_SEND_BUF_DEFAULT + TCP_RECV_BUF_DEFAULT
        if inet_tcp_buf_bytes_owner(owner_tid) + def_total > TCP_PER_TID_BUF_MAX
            return -1
        val s = inet_tcp_alloc()
        if s < 0
            return -1
        val lp = tcp_alloc_port()
        if lp < 0
            tcp_sockets[s].in_use = 0
            return -1
        // Stamp owners[0] before the pre-connect resize_buf calls
        // so the budget clamp (Phase 2 chunk 2) sees a real owner;
        // alloc leaves owners_count = 0.
        inet_tcp_owners_set(s, owner_tid)
        // Apply any setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF) the caller
        // stashed pre-connect.  resize_buf no-ops below TCP_BUF_MIN
        // and clamps to TCP_BUF_MAX. Done here while state is still
        // CLOSED — once we transition to SYN_SENT, resize is locked.
        if sndbuf_override > 0
            inet_tcp_resize_buf(s, 0, sndbuf_override)
        if rcvbuf_override > 0
            inet_tcp_resize_buf(s, 1, rcvbuf_override)
        val iss = tcp_next_iss()
        tcp_sockets[s].local_port = lp
        tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip = remote_ip
        tcp_sockets[s].remote_port = remote_port
        inet_tcp_conn_hash_insert(s)
        tcp_sockets[s].iss = iss
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_una = iss
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt = iss + u32(1)
        tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt = u32(0)
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_wnd = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_SYN_SENT
        tcp_sockets[s].connect_waiter = caller_tid
        inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_SYN, iss, u32(0), 0)
        inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s)
        s


listen() entry — allocate a slot bound to `local_port` in LISTEN
state. Unlike connect(), listen() returns immediately; the caller
reuses the returned fd for accept(). Fails with -1 if the slot
pool is exhausted or a listen is already bound to that port.

`backlog` is capped at TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG (the array size); a
value <= 0 is normalised to 1, matching Linux's "0 → 1" rule
so a tiny but positive queue depth is always available.

    inet_tcp_begin_listen(owner_tid: int, local_port: int,
                          backlog: int,
                          sndbuf_override: int, rcvbuf_override: int,
                          reuseaddr: int) -> int
        if inet_tcp_find_listen(local_port) >= 0
            return -1
        // RFC 793 / 1122 §4.2.2.10 — refuse `bind()` (rolled into
        // `begin_listen` here) on a port still serving the 2MSL
        // TIME_WAIT for a prior connection unless `SO_REUSEADDR` is
        // set. Linux applies the same rule. Without this check
        // `SO_REUSEADDR` was informational (per shim.lsysl §"slix is
        // already permissive") and a server restart could silently
        // accept a stale lingering segment as part of a brand-new
        // connection.
        if reuseaddr == 0 && inet_tcp_find_port_post_listen(local_port) >= 0
            return -1
        // Phase 2 quality plan: per-tid cap.  See begin_connect.
        if inet_count_tcp_owner(owner_tid) >= 8
            return -1
        // Phase 2 chunk 2: per-tid TCP buffer-memory cap.
        val def_total = TCP_SEND_BUF_DEFAULT + TCP_RECV_BUF_DEFAULT
        if inet_tcp_buf_bytes_owner(owner_tid) + def_total > TCP_PER_TID_BUF_MAX
            return -1
        val s = inet_tcp_alloc()
        if s < 0
            return -1
        // See begin_connect — owners[0] stamped before resize so
        // the budget clamp has a real owner to count against.
        inet_tcp_owners_set(s, owner_tid)
        // Pre-listen setsockopt sizing — see begin_connect.  The
        // listening socket carries its sizes through to children
        // in handle_passive_syn (Linux semantics: accepted child
        // inherits the listener's SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF).
        if sndbuf_override > 0
            inet_tcp_resize_buf(s, 0, sndbuf_override)
        if rcvbuf_override > 0
            inet_tcp_resize_buf(s, 1, rcvbuf_override)
        var b = backlog
        if b <= 0
            b = 1
        if b > TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG
            b = TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG
        tcp_sockets[s].local_port = local_port
        tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip = u32(0)
        tcp_sockets[s].remote_port = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_LISTEN
        tcp_sockets[s].is_listen = 1
        tcp_sockets[s].accept_backlog = b
        inet_tcp_listen_hash_insert(s)
        s


Handle an incoming SYN that matches no existing 4-tuple but does
match a LISTEN socket. Allocates a child socket in SYN_RCVD,
emits SYN-ACK, arms retransmit. The child is NOT queued for
accept() until its SYN_RCVD → ESTABLISHED transition.

    inet_tcp_handle_passive_syn(ls: int, remote_ip: u32,
                                remote_port: int, seg_seq: u32,
                                peer_sack_perm: int,
                                peer_has_ws: int, ws_shift: int,
                                peer_has_ts: int, ts_val: u32)
        // If accept queue is already at capacity, silently drop.
        // SYN cookies / overflow handling is phase-3. The peer's
        // SYN retransmit will retry once a slot frees up.
        if tcp_sockets[ls].accept_count >= tcp_sockets[ls].accept_backlog
            return
        // Phase 2 chunk 1/2 — apply per-tid slot AND buffer caps
        // on accept paths too. A misbehaving peer otherwise drives
        // unbounded child sockets through a single listener. Drop
        // silently; the SYN will be retransmitted once budget
        // frees up. Owner of the new child = listener's primary
        // owner.
        val parent_tid = inet_tcp_owners_primary(ls)
        if inet_count_tcp_owner(parent_tid) >= 8
            return
        val def_total = TCP_SEND_BUF_DEFAULT + TCP_RECV_BUF_DEFAULT
        if inet_tcp_buf_bytes_owner(parent_tid) + def_total > TCP_PER_TID_BUF_MAX
            return
        val s = inet_tcp_alloc()
        if s < 0
            return
        // Stamp owner before inherit-resize so the budget clamp
        // (Phase 2 chunk 2) sees the right tid; resize will
        // silently shrink the inherit if parent's sizes don't fit.
        inet_tcp_owners_set(s, parent_tid)
        // Inherit the listener's per-socket buffer sizes (Linux:
        // accepted children carry the listening socket's
        // SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF). resize_buf no-ops when the size
        // already matches, so default-sized listeners pay nothing.
        val parent_sndbuf = tcp_sockets[ls].send_buf_size
        val parent_rcvbuf = tcp_sockets[ls].recv_buf_size
        if parent_sndbuf != tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size
            inet_tcp_resize_buf(s, 0, parent_sndbuf)
        if parent_rcvbuf != tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size
            inet_tcp_resize_buf(s, 1, parent_rcvbuf)
        // Inherit SO_KEEPALIVE — Linux semantics: accepted child
        // carries the listening socket's keepalive flag.  The
        // probe schedule starts fresh from accept (last_recv_ms
        // already points at "now" via alloc).
        if tcp_sockets[ls].keepalive_on != 0
            inet_tcp_set_keepalive(s, 1)
        // Per-socket TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT
        // overrides also inherit. 0 on either side just means
        // "use the global default", so copying through is a no-op
        // for a listener that never tuned these.
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_idle = tcp_sockets[ls].keepalive_idle
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_intvl = tcp_sockets[ls].keepalive_intvl
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_cnt = tcp_sockets[ls].keepalive_cnt
        // Inherit TCP_USER_TIMEOUT (RFC 5482).  Linux propagates the
        // listener's timeout to passive opens just like the keepalive
        // options; copying 0 through is a no-op (the listener never
        // tuned it, so the child stays uncapped).
        tcp_sockets[s].user_timeout_ms = tcp_sockets[ls].user_timeout_ms
        val iss = tcp_next_iss()
        tcp_sockets[s].local_port = tcp_sockets[ls].local_port
        tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip = remote_ip
        tcp_sockets[s].remote_port = remote_port
        inet_tcp_conn_hash_insert(s)
        tcp_sockets[s].iss = iss
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_una = iss
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt = iss + u32(1)
        tcp_sockets[s].irs = seg_seq
        tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt = seg_seq + u32(1)
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_wnd = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_SYN_RCVD
        tcp_sockets[s].parent_listen = ls
        // RFC 2018 §2: enable SACK on this connection iff the
        // peer included SACK-permitted in its SYN. Our SYN-ACK
        // will echo SACK-permitted back (inet_tcp_emit always
        // includes it on any SYN frame).
        if peer_sack_perm != 0
            tcp_sockets[s].sack_ok = 1
        // RFC 7323 §1.3: WS is enabled only if both sides
        // advertised it. We always offer; mirror peer's shift
        // for inbound and set our own (TCP_RCV_WSCALE) for the
        // SYN-ACK we're about to emit.
        if peer_has_ws != 0
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_wscale = ws_shift
            tcp_sockets[s].rcv_wscale = TCP_RCV_WSCALE
        // RFC 7323 §1.3: TS is enabled only if both sides
        // advertised it. Seed `ts_recent` from the SYN's TSval
        // so the SYN-ACK echoes a non-zero TSecr.
        if peer_has_ts != 0
            tcp_sockets[s].ts_ok = 1
            tcp_sockets[s].ts_recent = ts_val
        inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_SYN | TCP_F_ACK, iss, u32(0), 0)
        inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s)


8-byte reply to a parked accept() caller:
status(1) + child_fd(1) + peer_ip(4) + peer_port(2).

    inet_tcp_reply_accept(tid: int, status: int, child_s: int)
        var wr: [8]byte
        wr[0] = byte(status & 0xff)
        wr[1] = byte(child_s & 0xff)
        if status == 0
            val rip = tcp_sockets[child_s].remote_ip
            wr[2] = byte(ipv4_octet(rip, 0) & 0xff)
            wr[3] = byte(ipv4_octet(rip, 1) & 0xff)
            wr[4] = byte(ipv4_octet(rip, 2) & 0xff)
            wr[5] = byte(ipv4_octet(rip, 3) & 0xff)
            pkt_write_u16(wr, 6, tcp_sockets[child_s].remote_port)
        ipc_reply(tid, wr, 8)


accept() entry — dequeue one established child (skipping dead
entries left behind by retransmit-giveup on a half-open child).
Returns the child socket index (caller replies immediately) or
-1 if the caller was parked as accept_waiter. Returns -2 if
`ls` is not a valid listen socket.

A child is also acceptable in CLOSE_WAIT: the peer can send data
and FIN while our accept() hasn't been called yet, so the child
transitions ESTABLISHED → CLOSE_WAIT before dequeue. Returning
it lets the caller drain buffered data and close cleanly instead
of silently dropping the connection. Other teardown states
(FIN_WAIT_*, LAST_ACK, TIME_WAIT) can't appear here — they only
enter after *we* close(), which the caller hasn't done yet.

    inet_tcp_try_accept(ls: int) -> int
        if tcp_sockets[ls].in_use == 0 || tcp_sockets[ls].is_listen == 0
            return -2
        while tcp_sockets[ls].accept_count > 0
            val head = tcp_sockets[ls].accept_head
            val c = tcp_sockets[ls].accept_queue[head]
            tcp_sockets[ls].accept_head = (head + 1) % TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG
            tcp_sockets[ls].accept_count -= 1
            if tcp_sockets[c].in_use != 0
                val cst = tcp_sockets[c].state
                if cst == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED || cst == TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
                    // Sever the parent link now that userspace owns the
                    // child fd. `do_close LISTEN` walks every slot with
                    // `parent_listen == ls` and force-frees it; without
                    // this clear, an accepted child whose userspace
                    // fd later goes through close() (FIN_WAIT_* / LAST_ACK /
                    // TIME_WAIT) would be torn down when the listener
                    // closes — wiping out the legitimate TIME_WAIT slot
                    // that `inet_tcp_find_time_wait` relies on.
                    tcp_sockets[c].parent_listen = -1
                    return c
        -1

    inet_tcp_do_accept(caller_tid: int, ls: int) -> int
        val r = inet_tcp_try_accept(ls)
        if r == -1
            // Queue empty — park caller; state_step delivers when a
            // SYN_RCVD child reaches ESTABLISHED.
            tcp_sockets[ls].accept_waiter = caller_tid
        r


Enqueue a newly-established child onto its parent listen's accept
queue. If a caller is already parked in accept(), wake it with the
child directly (the entry is dequeued before reply, not after).

    inet_tcp_accept_enqueue(child_s: int)
        val ls = tcp_sockets[child_s].parent_listen
        if ls < 0 || tcp_sockets[ls].in_use == 0 || tcp_sockets[ls].is_listen == 0
            return
        if tcp_sockets[ls].accept_waiter >= 0
            val tid = tcp_sockets[ls].accept_waiter
            tcp_sockets[ls].accept_waiter = -1
            inet_tcp_reply_accept(tid, 0, child_s)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[ls].accept_count >= tcp_sockets[ls].accept_backlog
            // Backlog full — the child becomes an orphan;
            // nobody will accept() it. Close it.
            inet_tcp_close_slot(child_s, -1)
            return
        val tail = tcp_sockets[ls].accept_tail
        tcp_sockets[ls].accept_queue[tail] = child_s
        tcp_sockets[ls].accept_tail = (tail + 1) % TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG
        tcp_sockets[ls].accept_count += 1
        inet_epoll_fire(2, ls)


Stateless RST emit for a segment arriving to a port with no
matching connection or listen socket. Per RFC 793 §3.4: if the
incoming segment has an ACK, the RST takes its seq from seg.ack
and no ACK flag; otherwise the RST has seq=0, ACK=seg.seq+seg_len,
and the ACK flag is set. seg_len counts SYN and FIN as one octet.

    inet_tcp_emit_rst(remote_ip: u32, remote_port: int,
                      local_port: int, seg_flags: int,
                      seg_seq: u32, seg_ack: u32, seg_payload_len: int)
        if inet_our_mac_known == 0
            return
        val is_loopback = inet_is_loopback_ip(remote_ip)
        var next_hop_mac: [6]byte
        if is_loopback != 0
            var mi = 0
            while mi < 6
                next_hop_mac[mi] = inet_our_mac[mi]
                mi += 1
        else
            if inet_resolve_mac(remote_ip, next_hop_mac) == 0
                return
        var rst_seq: u32 = u32(0)
        var rst_ack: u32 = u32(0)
        var rst_flags = TCP_F_RST
        if (seg_flags & TCP_F_ACK) != 0
            rst_seq = seg_ack
        else
            var ack_bump = seg_payload_len
            if (seg_flags & TCP_F_SYN) != 0
                ack_bump += 1
            if (seg_flags & TCP_F_FIN) != 0
                ack_bump += 1
            rst_ack = seg_seq + u32(ack_bump)
            rst_flags = TCP_F_RST | TCP_F_ACK
        val hdr_bytes = 20
        val seg_total = hdr_bytes
        val fidx = inet_frame_alloc()
        val frame = inet_frame_buf(fidx)
        val total_len = 14 + 20 + seg_total
        pkt_write_mac(frame, 0, next_hop_mac)
        pkt_write_mac(frame, 6, inet_our_mac)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 12, ETHERTYPE_IPV4)
        frame[14] = 0x45
        frame[15] = 0
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 16, 20 + seg_total)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 18, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 20, 0x4000)
        frame[22] = 64
        frame[23] = byte(IP_PROTO_TCP)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 24, 0)
        val our_ip = ipv4(INET_OUR_IP_A, INET_OUR_IP_B,
            INET_OUR_IP_C, INET_OUR_IP_D)
        pkt_write_ipv4(frame, 26,
            INET_OUR_IP_A, INET_OUR_IP_B, INET_OUR_IP_C, INET_OUR_IP_D)
        pkt_write_ipv4(frame, 30,
            ipv4_octet(remote_ip, 0), ipv4_octet(remote_ip, 1),
            ipv4_octet(remote_ip, 2), ipv4_octet(remote_ip, 3))
        pkt_write_u16(frame, 24, net_checksum(frame + 14, 20))
        val tcp_off = 34
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 0, local_port)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 2, remote_port)
        tcp_write_u32(frame, tcp_off + 4, rst_seq)
        tcp_write_u32(frame, tcp_off + 8, rst_ack)
        frame[tcp_off + 12] = byte((5 << 4) & 0xf0)
        frame[tcp_off + 13] = byte(rst_flags & 0xff)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 14, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 16, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 18, 0)
        pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 16,
            (~inet_l4_partial_sum(our_ip, remote_ip,
                IP_PROTO_TCP, frame, tcp_off, seg_total)) & 0xffff)
        if is_loopback != 0
            // Same swap-and-recompute pattern as inet_tcp_emit.
            // RST is terminal, so no further recursion downstream.
            var sw = 0
            while sw < 4
                val tmp = frame[26 + sw]
                frame[26 + sw] = frame[30 + sw]
                frame[30 + sw] = tmp
                sw += 1
            pkt_write_u16(frame, 24, 0)
            pkt_write_u16(frame, 24, net_checksum(frame + 14, 20))
            val src_in = ipv4(int(frame[26]) & 0xff, int(frame[27]) & 0xff,
                int(frame[28]) & 0xff, int(frame[29]) & 0xff)
            val dst_in = ipv4(int(frame[30]) & 0xff, int(frame[31]) & 0xff,
                int(frame[32]) & 0xff, int(frame[33]) & 0xff)
            pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 16, 0)
            pkt_write_u16(frame, tcp_off + 16,
                (~inet_l4_partial_sum(src_in, dst_in,
                    IP_PROTO_TCP, frame, tcp_off, seg_total)) & 0xffff)
            inet_tcp_handle_segment(frame, total_len, 20)
            inet_frame_release(fidx)
            return
        inet_nic_send_packet(frame, total_len)
        inet_frame_release(fidx)


RFC 5681 congestion-window growth. Called whenever an ACK
advances `snd_una`. `acked` is the number of data bytes newly
acknowledged (`eff` in the caller — so a pure FIN ACK, which
has `eff = 0`, is a no-op here).

- In slow start (`cwnd < ssthresh`): grow `cwnd` by at most
  one SMSS per cumulative ACK (RFC 5681 §3.1 ¶2). Matches the
  classical doubling-per-RTT behaviour.
- In congestion avoidance (`cwnd >= ssthresh`): grow `cwnd`
  by `SMSS * SMSS / cwnd` per ACK, rounded up to at least 1
  so we always make forward progress. Net effect is ~one
  SMSS per RTT under steady cumulative-ACK cadence.

    inet_tcp_cwnd_on_ack(s: int, acked: int)
        if acked <= 0
            return
        var cwnd = tcp_sockets[s].snd_cwnd
        if cwnd < tcp_sockets[s].snd_ssthresh
            // Slow start. +1 SMSS per ACK, but capped at the
            // bytes actually acked to avoid over-inflating on
            // a tiny tail ACK.
            var inc = TCP_MAX_SEG
            if inc > acked
                inc = acked
            cwnd += inc
        else
            var inc = (TCP_MAX_SEG * TCP_MAX_SEG) / cwnd
            if inc < 1
                inc = 1
            cwnd += inc
        // Runaway guard. 64 KB is far beyond anything our
        // 1024-byte send_buf will ever exercise; acts purely
        // as a sanity clamp so misbehaving peers can't make
        // the cwnd integer saturate.
        if cwnd > 65535
            cwnd = 65535
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_cwnd = cwnd


RFC 5681 §3.1 ¶3 — RTO timeout collapse. Called from
`inet_tcp_scan_timers` before retransmit. `FlightSize` is the
bytes in flight (`snd_nxt - snd_una`) at the moment of loss;
`ssthresh` is reset to `max(FlightSize / 2, 2 * SMSS)`,
`cwnd` collapses to one SMSS (our `TCP_MIN_CWND`).

Only applies when we have actual in-flight data — SYN / FIN
retransmits keep their current cwnd, which is fine because
those are handshake probes and cwnd isn't meaningful for them
yet.

    inet_tcp_cwnd_on_rto(s: int)
        val in_flight = int(i32(
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt - tcp_sockets[s].snd_una))
        if in_flight <= 0
            return
        var new_ssthresh = in_flight / 2
        val floor = 2 * TCP_MAX_SEG
        if new_ssthresh < floor
            new_ssthresh = floor
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_ssthresh = new_ssthresh
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_cwnd = TCP_MIN_CWND
        // RTO supersedes fast recovery — we're clearly losing
        // more than fast retransmit assumed, so reset to slow
        // start from a single MSS.
        tcp_sockets[s].fast_recovery = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].dup_ack_count = 0
        // Any SACK info the peer had is worthless now: RTO means
        // we can't trust the scoreboard was coherent with what's
        // still in flight.
        tcp_sack_board_clear(s)


RFC 5681 §3.2 ¶4-5 — fast retransmit + enter fast recovery.
Fired on the third duplicate ACK. Halves `ssthresh` from the
current `FlightSize` (floored at 2 × SMSS), **inflates**
`cwnd` to `ssthresh + 3 × SMSS` to account for the three
segments that must have left the network to elicit the three
dup-ACKs, and sets `fast_recovery = 1`. `recovery_point`
records `snd_nxt` at entry so the ACK handler knows when
recovery ends (first ACK past this seq).

Retransmits one MSS-sized segment. Without SACK we have to
assume the dropped segment is at `snd_una` — peer hasn't told
us anything finer. With SACK live we point the retransmit at
the first real hole (`tcp_sack_next_hole`) so the first
retransmit carries data the peer is actually missing.

`snd_nxt` is NOT reset — any segments past the retransmit
stay in-flight and drain handles window accounting correctly.

    inet_tcp_fast_retransmit(s: int)
        val in_flight = int(i32(
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt - tcp_sockets[s].snd_una))
        var new_ssthresh = in_flight / 2
        val floor = 2 * TCP_MAX_SEG
        if new_ssthresh < floor
            new_ssthresh = floor
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_ssthresh = new_ssthresh
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_cwnd = new_ssthresh + 3 * TCP_MAX_SEG
        tcp_sockets[s].fast_recovery = 1
        tcp_sockets[s].recovery_point = tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
        if tcp_sockets[s].send_len > 0
            var retx_seq = tcp_sockets[s].snd_una
            if tcp_sockets[s].sack_ok != 0
                val hole = tcp_sack_next_hole(s, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una)
                if tcp_seq_lt(hole, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt) != 0
                    retx_seq = hole
            val avail = int(i32(tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt - retx_seq))
            var retx_len = avail
            if retx_len > TCP_MAX_SEG
                retx_len = TCP_MAX_SEG
            val pmtu_cap = inet_tcp_eff_mss(s)
            if retx_len > pmtu_cap
                retx_len = pmtu_cap
            if retx_len > 0
                inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_PSH,
                    retx_seq, retx_seq, retx_len)
                tcp_sockets[s].sack_rexmit_cursor = retx_seq + u32(retx_len)
                tcp_sockets[s].sack_rexmit_valid = 1
                tcp_sockets[s].rexmit_count += 1
                inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s)


Issue one more SACK-guided retransmit, picking up where the
last one left off. Called on partial ACKs inside fast recovery
and after SACK-driven cwnd inflation so additional holes get
plugged promptly instead of waiting for RTO.

    inet_tcp_sack_rexmit_next(s: int)
        if tcp_sockets[s].sack_ok == 0
            return
        if tcp_sockets[s].send_len == 0
            return
        var cursor = tcp_sockets[s].snd_una
        if tcp_sockets[s].sack_rexmit_valid != 0
            if tcp_seq_lt(tcp_sockets[s].sack_rexmit_cursor, cursor) == 0
                cursor = tcp_sockets[s].sack_rexmit_cursor
        val hole = tcp_sack_next_hole(s, cursor)
        if tcp_seq_lt(hole, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt) == 0
            return
        val avail = int(i32(tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt - hole))
        var retx_len = avail
        if retx_len > TCP_MAX_SEG
            retx_len = TCP_MAX_SEG
        val pmtu_cap = inet_tcp_eff_mss(s)
        if retx_len > pmtu_cap
            retx_len = pmtu_cap
        if retx_len <= 0
            return
        inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_PSH,
            hole, hole, retx_len)
        tcp_sockets[s].sack_rexmit_cursor = hole + u32(retx_len)
        tcp_sockets[s].sack_rexmit_valid = 1
        tcp_sockets[s].rexmit_count += 1
        inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s)


Pump as many segments out of `send_buf` as the effective send
window allows. Called from `inet_tcp_do_send` after buffering
new user data, and from the ACK path after `snd_una` advances
(to exploit the window that just opened).

Effective window = `min(snd_cwnd, snd_wnd)` — standard TCP
(RFC 5681 §2). `snd_wnd` is the receiver's advertised rwnd;
`snd_cwnd` is our own congestion window. In-flight bytes are
`snd_nxt - snd_una`; room is `effective_window - in_flight`.

Each segment is at most `TCP_MAX_SEG` bytes (our SMSS). Stops
when the window is full, when `send_buf` has no more unsent
bytes, or when `snd_wnd` is zero (peer closed its window).

RTT sample anchors on the last segment's tail sequence — good
enough for coarse RTT tracking.

    inet_tcp_drain_send(s: int)
        val st = tcp_sockets[s].state
        if st != TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED && st != TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
            return
        var window = tcp_sockets[s].snd_cwnd
        if tcp_sockets[s].snd_wnd < window
            window = tcp_sockets[s].snd_wnd
        var emitted_any = 0
        while 1 == 1
            val in_flight = int(i32(
                tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt - tcp_sockets[s].snd_una))
            val unsent = tcp_sockets[s].send_len - in_flight
            if unsent <= 0
                break
            val room = window - in_flight
            if room <= 0
                break
            var seg_size = unsent
            if seg_size > TCP_MAX_SEG
                seg_size = TCP_MAX_SEG
            val pmtu_cap = inet_tcp_eff_mss(s)
            if seg_size > pmtu_cap
                seg_size = pmtu_cap
            if seg_size > room
                seg_size = room
            val seq = tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_PSH, seq, seq, seg_size)
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt = seq + u32(seg_size)
            emitted_any = 1
        if emitted_any != 0
            inet_tcp_rtt_start_sample(s, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt)
            inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s)


send() entry — append to `send_buf` and drain as much as the
congestion + receive windows allow. Returns bytes accepted
(>= 0), -1 if the connection isn't open for sending.

CLOSE_WAIT (peer half-closed their write side) is still fine
for sending: our read side has EOF'd but not our write side.
Returns 0 if the send buffer is full — caller must wait for an
ACK and retry.

    inet_tcp_do_send(s: int, data: *byte, len: int) -> int
        val ds_st = tcp_sockets[s].state
        if ds_st != TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED && ds_st != TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
            return -1
        val space = tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size - tcp_sockets[s].send_len
        var n = len
        if n > space
            n = space
        if n <= 0
            return 0
        val start_off = tcp_sockets[s].send_len
        var i = 0
        while i < n
            tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_ptr[start_off + i] = data[i]
            i += 1
        // Anchor the TCP_USER_TIMEOUT clock on the 0→>0 transition.
        // RFC 5482 §3: the user-timeout countdown begins when the
        // connection first has unacked data; subsequent appends within
        // an already-unacked run inherit the original anchor so a
        // steady stream of small writes can't reset the timer.
        if start_off == 0
            tcp_sockets[s].earliest_unack_ms = int(monotonic_ms())
        tcp_sockets[s].send_len += n
        inet_tcp_drain_send(s)
        n


close() entry — emit FIN (or immediately free if never opened).
Returns 0 if FIN is in-flight (caller blocks), 1 if already done,
-1 on invalid state.

    inet_tcp_do_close(s: int, caller_tid: int) -> int
        val st = tcp_sockets[s].state
        if st == TCP_STATE_CLOSED
            tcp_sockets[s].in_use = 0
            return 1
        if st == TCP_STATE_LISTEN
            // Release every child that was created via this listen —
            // queued-but-not-yet-accepted ESTABLISHED sockets, and
            // SYN_RCVD children still mid-handshake. Without this they
            // orphan onto dead parent_listen and leak their slots
            // until peer-driven teardown (which may never arrive).
            // Note: `inet_tcp_try_accept` clears `parent_listen = -1`
            // on dequeue, so accepted children survive listener close
            // (matches Linux semantics) and can outlive the listener
            // through their own teardown to TIME_WAIT.
            var i = 0
            while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
                if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                    if tcp_sockets[i].parent_listen == s
                        inet_tcp_close_slot(i, -1)
                i += 1
            // No peer to FIN; any parked accept_waiter gets woken
            // with status=1 (error) by close_slot.
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 1)
            return 1
        if st == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED
            val seq = tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt = seq + u32(1)
            tcp_sockets[s].our_fin_sent = 1
            tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_1
            // Linux semantics: close() returns to userspace immediately;
            // the kernel drives FIN/FIN-ACK and TIME_WAIT in the
            // background. SO_LINGER (l_onoff=1, l_linger>0) would park
            // the caller here on close_waiter and arm a linger deadline
            // — not yet wired; SO_LINGER l_onoff=1 + l_linger=0 already
            // takes the abort-close path via INET_CMD_TCP_ABORT_CLOSE.
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_FIN, seq, seq, 0)
            // Only arm rtx if our FIN is still unacked. On the
            // loopback fastpath, `inet_tcp_emit` synchronously drives
            // cl ESTABLISHED → CLOSE_WAIT with a piggyback ACK, then
            // ch state_step processes that ACK and `disarm_rtx`s
            // (line 5020-5021) once snd_una catches up. Without this
            // gate, we'd re-arm a timer with nothing to retransmit;
            // the scan would still fire it `TCP_MAX_RETRIES` times
            // and tear the slot down out of FIN_WAIT_2 instead of
            // letting it reach TIME_WAIT.
            if tcp_seq_lt(tcp_sockets[s].snd_una, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt) != 0
                inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s)
            return 1
        if st == TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
            val seq = tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt = seq + u32(1)
            tcp_sockets[s].our_fin_sent = 1
            tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_LAST_ACK
            // Linux semantics: same as ESTABLISHED above — return
            // immediately, drain async via state_step and scan_timers.
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_FIN, seq, seq, 0)
            // Same gate as ESTABLISHED above — loopback's synchronous
            // ACK can carry us past LAST_ACK before we'd ever need a
            // retransmit timer.
            if tcp_seq_lt(tcp_sockets[s].snd_una, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt) != 0
                inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s)
            return 1
        if st == TCP_STATE_SYN_SENT
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, -1)
            return 1
        -1


Reclaim every TCP slot whose owners ring contains a terminated
thread. Called from the PID_EXIT IPC path when PM notifies inet
that a process is dying. Children of a listen() inherit the
listener's primary owner at passive-open time, so the same loop
catches queued-but-not-yet-accepted connections.

Multi-owner: just removing `tid` from the ring is the right move
when it isn't the last owner — another live thread still has a
shim slot pointing at this socket, and tearing it down would
orphan that fd. Only when the ring empties do we close the slot.
Ungraceful close via `inet_tcp_close_slot` — peers of ESTABLISHED
sockets won't see a FIN (they'd get one had the process called
close() itself); a graceful variant that sends RST or drives FIN
through the state machine is a future refinement.

    inet_tcp_cleanup_owner(tid: int)
        var i = 0
        while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_tcp_owners_has(i, tid) != 0
                    val left = inet_tcp_owners_remove(i, tid)
                    if left == 0
                        // Phase 2B chunk 3: defer teardown while an
                        // in-flight SCM cmsg holds a queue ref. The
                        // matching QUEUE_REF(-1) reaches the deferred
                        // close path in `inet_handle_queue_ref`.
                        if tcp_sockets[i].queued_refs == 0
                            // Try graceful close first — emits FIN and
                            // lets the state machine + scan_timers
                            // handle the rest. do_close returns -1 for
                            // states it can't handle (SYN_RCVD,
                            // FIN_WAIT_*, LAST_ACK, TIME_WAIT); fall
                            // back to force-free for those. caller_tid
                            // = -1 so no waiter is parked on
                            // close_waiter.
                            val r = inet_tcp_do_close(i, -1)
                            if r < 0
                                inet_tcp_close_slot(i, -1)
            i += 1


Combined fd/ownership check for the TCP handlers. Returns 1 when
the slot is in-range, in-use, AND `sender` is in the owners ring;
0 otherwise. Replaces the earlier pattern of "bounds check +
in_use check" — adds the missing ownership guard the
`inet_fd_valid` memo flagged. Multi-owner-aware: any tid in the
slot's owners ring passes (added in Phase 2A of the SCM_RIGHTS
work).

    inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd: int, sender: int) -> int
        if fd < 0
            return 0
        if fd >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return 0
        if tcp_sockets[fd].in_use == 0
            return 0
        if inet_tcp_owners_has(fd, sender) == 0
            return 0
        1


Half-close the write side of socket `s`. Transitions ESTABLISHED
→ FIN_WAIT_1 (or CLOSE_WAIT → LAST_ACK) and emits our FIN, but
does NOT set `close_waiter` — the caller is not parked, and the
read side remains readable until the peer FINs. The blocking
path (inet_tcp_do_close) would otherwise block shutdown() the
same as close(), which isn't what POSIX wants for SHUT_WR.

Idempotent: returns 0 in every case, including already-shutdown
states (the caller shouldn't care if the FIN was a no-op).

    inet_tcp_do_shutdown_wr(s: int) -> int
        val st = tcp_sockets[s].state
        if tcp_sockets[s].our_fin_sent != 0
            return 0
        if st == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED
            val seq = tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt = seq + u32(1)
            tcp_sockets[s].our_fin_sent = 1
            tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_1
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_FIN, seq, seq, 0)
            inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s)
            return 0
        if st == TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
            val seq = tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt = seq + u32(1)
            tcp_sockets[s].our_fin_sent = 1
            tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_LAST_ACK
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_FIN, seq, seq, 0)
            inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s)
            return 0
        0


Query the local or peer endpoint of a TCP socket. `which == 0`
asks for the local side (always `inet_get_our_ip()`:local_port);
`which != 0` asks for the peer side and requires the socket to
have reached ESTABLISHED or later — returns -ENOTCONN otherwise.
Caller-supplied out parameters receive the port (host order) and
IP (u32, network byte order via `ipv4(...)`). Returns 0 on
success, -1 for an invalid fd, -107 (-ENOTCONN) for an
unconnected peer query.

    inet_tcp_getname(fd: int, which: int, out_port: *int, out_ip: *u32) -> int
        if fd < 0
            return -1
        if fd >= TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            return -1
        if tcp_sockets[fd].in_use == 0
            return -1
        if which == 0
            *out_port = tcp_sockets[fd].local_port
            *out_ip = inet_get_our_ip()
            return 0
        val st = tcp_sockets[fd].state
        if st != TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED && st != TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT && st != TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_1 && st != TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_2 && st != TCP_STATE_LAST_ACK && st != TCP_STATE_TIME_WAIT
            return -107
        *out_port = tcp_sockets[fd].remote_port
        *out_ip = tcp_sockets[fd].remote_ip
        0


Inbound IPv4 protocol=6 dispatch. Parses header, verifies
checksum, locates the matching socket, and delegates.

    inet_tcp_handle_segment(frame: *byte, total_len: int, ihl_bytes: int)
        val tcp_off = 14 + ihl_bytes
        if total_len < tcp_off + 20
            return
        val src_port = pkt_read_u16(frame, tcp_off + 0)
        val dst_port = pkt_read_u16(frame, tcp_off + 2)
        val seq = tcp_read_u32(frame, tcp_off + 4)
        val ack = tcp_read_u32(frame, tcp_off + 8)
        val data_off_words = (int(frame[tcp_off + 12]) >> 4) & 0x0f
        val tcp_hdr_len = data_off_words * 4
        val flags = int(frame[tcp_off + 13]) & 0xff
        val wnd = pkt_read_u16(frame, tcp_off + 14)
        if tcp_hdr_len < 20
            return
        if total_len < tcp_off + tcp_hdr_len
            return
        val ip_total = pkt_read_u16(frame, 16)
        val tcp_len = ip_total - ihl_bytes
        val payload_len = tcp_len - tcp_hdr_len
        val payload: *byte = frame + tcp_off + tcp_hdr_len
        val src_ip = ipv4(
            int(frame[14 + 12]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 13]) & 0xff,
            int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff)
        val our_ip = ipv4(INET_OUR_IP_A, INET_OUR_IP_B,
            INET_OUR_IP_C, INET_OUR_IP_D)
        if inet_l4_partial_sum(src_ip, our_ip, IP_PROTO_TCP,
                frame, tcp_off, tcp_len) != 0xffff
            return
        // RFC 2018 SACK-permitted is negotiated once on the
        // handshake; parse it here so both the passive-open and
        // state-step paths can see the peer's offer.
        val peer_sack_perm = tcp_opts_has_sack_permitted(
            frame, tcp_off, tcp_hdr_len)
        // RFC 7323 WS + TS — also handshake-negotiated, but TS
        // appears on every segment once enabled so we parse on
        // every inbound and let state_step decide.
        var ws_shift = 0
        val peer_has_ws = tcp_opts_get_wscale(frame, tcp_off,
            tcp_hdr_len, ws_shift)
        var ts_val: u32 = u32(0)
        var ts_ecr: u32 = u32(0)
        val peer_has_ts = tcp_opts_get_ts(frame, tcp_off,
            tcp_hdr_len, ts_val, ts_ecr)
        val s = inet_tcp_find(dst_port, src_ip, src_port)
        if s < 0
            // No connection match. SYN-to-a-listen gets the passive
            // open path. Anything else that isn't already a RST gets
            // a stateless RST per RFC 793 §3.4.
            if (flags & TCP_F_RST) != 0
                return
            if (flags & TCP_F_SYN) != 0
                val ls = inet_tcp_find_listen(dst_port)
                if ls >= 0
                    inet_tcp_handle_passive_syn(ls, src_ip, src_port,
                        seq, peer_sack_perm, peer_has_ws, ws_shift,
                        peer_has_ts, ts_val)
                    return
            inet_tcp_emit_rst(src_ip, src_port, dst_port,
                              flags, seq, ack, payload_len)
            return
        // RFC 2018 §4: consult the SACK option on ACKs so the sender
        // scoreboard stays current. Only meaningful once both sides
        // agreed on SACK-permitted — otherwise the peer's option
        // field is garbage we shouldn't trust.
        if tcp_sockets[s].sack_ok != 0
            if (flags & TCP_F_ACK) != 0
                tcp_opts_apply_sack(s, frame, tcp_off, tcp_hdr_len)
        // SO_KEEPALIVE: any inbound segment for this connection
        // resets the idle clock, so an active flow never trips the
        // probe path.  Bumping unconditionally (not just on data) is
        // what Linux does — a peer ACK on idle counts as liveness.
        tcp_sockets[s].last_recv_ms = int(monotonic_ms())
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_probes = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].keepalive_next_probe = 0
        inet_tcp_state_step(s, flags, seq, ack, wnd, payload,
            payload_len, peer_sack_perm, peer_has_ws, ws_shift,
            peer_has_ts, ts_val, ts_ecr)


Reorder queue for out-of-order segments.

`inet_tcp_reorder_bytes` returns the total payload bytes currently
stashed in the reorder queue for socket `s`. Used by `inet_tcp_emit`
to subtract stashed bytes from the advertised receive window, and
by the stash window check so peer sees a consistent window.

    inet_tcp_reorder_bytes(s: int) -> int
        var total = 0
        var k = 0
        while k < TCP_REORDER_SLOTS
            if tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].in_use != 0
                total += tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].len
            k += 1
        total

`inet_tcp_reorder_store` stashes a segment whose seq > rcv_nxt but
still inside the advertised recv window. Duplicates (seq already
stored) and partial overlaps are rejected — peer will retransmit.
Returns 1 if stored, 0 if dropped.

    inet_tcp_reorder_store(s: int, seq: u32, payload: *byte,
                           payload_len: int) -> int
        if payload_len <= 0
            return 0
        if payload_len > TCP_MAX_SEG
            return 0
        var k = 0
        while k < TCP_REORDER_SLOTS
            if tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].in_use != 0
                val e_seq = tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].seq
                val e_end = e_seq + u32(tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].len)
                val n_end = seq + u32(payload_len)
                if tcp_seq_lt(seq, e_end) != 0
                    if tcp_seq_lt(e_seq, n_end) != 0
                        return 0
            k += 1
        var j = 0
        while j < TCP_REORDER_SLOTS
            if tcp_sockets[s].reorder[j].in_use == 0
                tcp_sockets[s].reorder[j].in_use = 1
                tcp_sockets[s].reorder[j].seq = seq
                tcp_sockets[s].reorder[j].len = payload_len
                var i = 0
                while i < payload_len
                    tcp_sockets[s].reorder[j].data[i] = payload[i]
                    i += 1
                return 1
            j += 1
        0

`inet_tcp_drain_reorder` is called after a contiguous append to
recv_buf. It scans the reorder queue for slots whose seq matches
the new rcv_nxt, copies their bytes into recv_buf, advances
rcv_nxt, and frees the slot. Continues until no slot lines up.
If a pending FIN (fin_pending != 0, fin_seq == rcv_nxt) lines up,
processes it too.

Returns non-zero if any data was drained (caller uses this to
decide whether an ACK needs to be re-emitted). Bit 0 = data,
bit 1 = FIN processed.

    inet_tcp_drain_reorder(s: int) -> int
        var result = 0
        var progress = 1
        while progress != 0
            progress = 0
            var k = 0
            while k < TCP_REORDER_SLOTS
                if tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].in_use != 0
                    if tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].seq == tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt
                        val take = tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].len
                        val room = tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size - tcp_sockets[s].recv_len
                        if take <= room
                            var i = 0
                            while i < take
                                tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr[tcp_sockets[s].recv_len + i] = tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].data[i]
                                i += 1
                            tcp_sockets[s].recv_len += take
                            tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt = tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt + u32(take)
                            tcp_sockets[s].reorder[k].in_use = 0
                            result = result | 1
                            progress = 1
                k += 1
            if tcp_sockets[s].fin_pending != 0
                if tcp_sockets[s].fin_seq == tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt
                    tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt = tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt + u32(1)
                    tcp_sockets[s].peer_fin = 1
                    tcp_sockets[s].fin_pending = 0
                    result = result | 2
        // One fire covers both newly-drained data and resolved FIN —
        // the shim's poll re-derives the actual mask either way.
        if result != 0
            inet_epoll_fire(2, s)
        result


Per-socket state transition for one validated segment.

    inet_tcp_state_step(s: int, flags: int, seq: u32, ack: u32,
                        wnd: int, payload: *byte, payload_len: int,
                        peer_sack_perm: int,
                        peer_has_ws: int, ws_shift: int,
                        peer_has_ts: int, ts_val: u32, ts_ecr: u32)
        val st = tcp_sockets[s].state
        if st == TCP_STATE_SYN_SENT
            if (flags & TCP_F_RST) != 0
                // RFC 793: RST in response to a SYN means the peer is
                // not listening (or refused). NB-connect surfaces this
                // as ECONNREFUSED via SO_ERROR after close_slot tears
                // down the slot.
                tcp_sockets[s].pending_error = 111
                inet_tcp_close_slot(s, -1)
                return
            if (flags & TCP_F_SYN) == 0
                return
            if (flags & TCP_F_ACK) == 0
                return
            if ack != tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
                return
            tcp_sockets[s].irs = seq
            tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt = seq + u32(1)
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_una = ack
            // RFC 7323 §2.2: SYN window field is unscaled. Apply
            // shift only after the SYN-ACK lands and we know the
            // peer's wscale.
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_wnd = wnd
            tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED
            // RFC 2018 §2: both our SYN and the peer's SYN-ACK
            // must carry SACK-permitted for SACK to be enabled
            // on this connection. We always advertise it in our
            // SYN, so the peer's SYN-ACK option is the deciding
            // factor.
            if peer_sack_perm != 0
                tcp_sockets[s].sack_ok = 1
            // RFC 7323 §1.3: WS / TS only stick if the peer
            // echoes them in the SYN-ACK. Otherwise our SYN's
            // offer is silently dropped — leave snd_wscale=0 and
            // ts_ok=0 (already initialised in alloc).
            if peer_has_ws != 0
                tcp_sockets[s].snd_wscale = ws_shift
                tcp_sockets[s].rcv_wscale = TCP_RCV_WSCALE
            if peer_has_ts != 0
                tcp_sockets[s].ts_ok = 1
                tcp_sockets[s].ts_recent = ts_val
            inet_tcp_disarm_rtx(s)
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt, u32(0), 0)
            if tcp_sockets[s].connect_waiter >= 0
                inet_tcp_reply_connect(tcp_sockets[s].connect_waiter, 0, s)
                tcp_sockets[s].connect_waiter = -1
            // Connect completed → EPOLLOUT becomes deliverable on the
            // socket (Linux semantics: writability transitions on the
            // SYN-ACK).
            inet_epoll_fire(2, s)
            return
        if st == TCP_STATE_SYN_RCVD
            if (flags & TCP_F_RST) != 0
                tcp_sockets[s].pending_error = 104  // ECONNRESET
                inet_tcp_close_slot(s, -1)
                return
            if (flags & TCP_F_ACK) == 0
                return
            if ack != tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
                return
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_una = ack
            // RFC 7323 §2.3: this is the first non-SYN segment from
            // the peer, so apply our negotiated `snd_wscale`.
            tcp_sockets[s].snd_wnd = wnd << tcp_sockets[s].snd_wscale
            tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED
            inet_tcp_disarm_rtx(s)
            // Hand child to parent listen (or directly to the parked
            // accept caller). Data in this segment is ignored — rely on
            // the peer retransmitting once we ACK the next segment.
            inet_tcp_accept_enqueue(s)
            return

TIME_WAIT is its own branch so the past-handshake logic below
doesn't second-guess a quiescent socket. We re-ACK retransmitted
FINs (peer missed our last ACK) and refresh the 2MSL timer so a
stream of FIN retransmits keeps absorbing; new-SYN-on-TIME_WAIT
(RFC 1122 §4.2.2.13) is silently dropped here — peer retries and
the listen path picks it up.

        if st == TCP_STATE_TIME_WAIT
            if (flags & TCP_F_RST) != 0
                inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
                return
            if (flags & TCP_F_FIN) != 0
                inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt, u32(0), 0)
                tcp_sockets[s].tw_deadline = int(monotonic_ms()) + TCP_TW_MS
            return

RFC 7323 §5.3 PAWS: a stale duplicate's TSval will be older than
the highest TSval we've recorded for the connection. If the gap is
big enough we drop the segment (but still ACK current rcv_nxt so
the peer doesn't keep retransmitting). The check only runs once
both sides have agreed on TS during the handshake.

After PAWS clears, advance `ts_recent` so subsequent retransmit
duplicates of this segment will be filtered. We use the loose
"any TSval ≥ recent" rule rather than the stricter §4.3 form
(SEG.SEQ ≤ Last.ACK.sent) — the difference only matters when
peer aggressively reorders.

        if tcp_sockets[s].ts_ok != 0
            if peer_has_ts != 0
                if tcp_seq_lt(ts_val, tcp_sockets[s].ts_recent) != 0
                    inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK,
                        tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt, u32(0), 0)
                    return
                if tcp_seq_lt(tcp_sockets[s].ts_recent, ts_val) != 0
                    tcp_sockets[s].ts_recent = ts_val

Past handshake: process ACK, data, FIN in that order.

        if (flags & TCP_F_RST) != 0
            tcp_sockets[s].pending_error = 104  // ECONNRESET
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, -1)
            return

RFC 5961 §4: a SYN arriving on a synchronized connection is a
challenge — respond with our current ACK and drop the segment.
Covers confused peers / port-collision retries without tearing
the connection down.

        if (flags & TCP_F_SYN) != 0
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt, u32(0), 0)
            return
        if (flags & TCP_F_ACK) != 0
            if tcp_seq_lt(tcp_sockets[s].snd_una, ack) != 0
                if tcp_seq_le(ack, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt) != 0
                    val acked = int(i32(ack - tcp_sockets[s].snd_una))
                    var eff = acked
                    if tcp_sockets[s].our_fin_sent != 0
                        if ack == tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
                            eff -= 1
                    if eff > tcp_sockets[s].send_len
                        eff = tcp_sockets[s].send_len
                    var k = eff
                    while k < tcp_sockets[s].send_len
                        tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_ptr[k - eff] = tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_ptr[k]
                        k += 1
                    tcp_sockets[s].send_len -= eff
                    tcp_sockets[s].snd_una = ack
                    // TCP_USER_TIMEOUT bookkeeping (RFC 5482 §3):
                    // any cumulative ACK is "progress" — restart the
                    // unack clock from this moment.  Once the unacked
                    // window fully drains (snd_una == snd_nxt) clear
                    // it so the timer doesn't fire on an idle
                    // connection waiting for the next write.
                    if tcp_sockets[s].snd_una == tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
                        tcp_sockets[s].earliest_unack_ms = 0
                    else
                        tcp_sockets[s].earliest_unack_ms = int(monotonic_ms())
                    // RFC 7323 §2.3: shift the raw header field by
                    // the negotiated peer scale.  Defaults to 0
                    // (no-op) when WS wasn't agreed on.
                    tcp_sockets[s].snd_wnd = wnd << tcp_sockets[s].snd_wscale
                    tcp_sockets[s].dup_ack_count = 0
                    // Send-buffer drained — wake any epoll waiter on
                    // EPOLLOUT. We over-fire (no full→drain check) on
                    // purpose; the shim filters via INET_CMD_POLL.
                    if eff > 0
                        inet_epoll_fire(2, s)
                        inet_tcp_drain_send_waiter(s)
                    // Cumulative ACK just swept some bytes — drop any
                    // scoreboard entries now wholly covered. Keeps
                    // `tcp_sack_next_hole` pointed at the right place
                    // without stale blocks lingering.
                    tcp_sack_board_purge(s)
                    // Prefer the TS-based RTT sample (RFC 7323 §4):
                    // it isn't ambiguous on retransmits the way Karn's
                    // sequence sampling is, so we get fresh
                    // measurements even after loss.  Falls back to
                    // Karn's algorithm when TS isn't negotiated.
                    if tcp_sockets[s].ts_ok != 0 && peer_has_ts != 0 && ts_ecr != u32(0)
                        val r = int(monotonic_ms()) - int(i32(ts_ecr - tcp_sockets[s].ts_offset))
                        if r >= 0 && r <= TCP_RTO_MS_MAX
                            tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_valid = 0
                            inet_tcp_rtt_update(s, r)
                    else
                        inet_tcp_rtt_maybe_sample(s, ack)
                    // RFC 5681 §3.2 ¶6 — recovering ACK: if we're
                    // in fast recovery and this ACK reaches beyond
                    // the recovery_point captured at entry, deflate
                    // cwnd back to ssthresh and leave recovery.
                    // Otherwise grow cwnd via the normal slow-start
                    // / CA path.
                    if tcp_sockets[s].fast_recovery != 0
                        if tcp_seq_le(tcp_sockets[s].recovery_point, ack) != 0
                            tcp_sockets[s].snd_cwnd = tcp_sockets[s].snd_ssthresh
                            tcp_sockets[s].fast_recovery = 0
                            tcp_sack_board_clear(s)
                        else
                            // Partial ACK inside recovery: if SACK is
                            // live, plug the next hole immediately
                            // instead of waiting for RTO (RFC 6675
                            // style NextSeg — slix's minimal form
                            // issues one hole per partial ACK).
                            if tcp_sockets[s].sack_ok != 0
                                inet_tcp_sack_rexmit_next(s)
                    else
                        inet_tcp_cwnd_on_ack(s, eff)
                    if tcp_sockets[s].snd_una == tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
                        inet_tcp_disarm_rtx(s)
                    else
                        inet_tcp_arm_rtx(s)
                    inet_tcp_drain_send(s)
            else if ack == tcp_sockets[s].snd_una
                // Potential duplicate ACK (RFC 5681 §2). Counts as
                // a "pure" dup only if there's no payload and we
                // still have outstanding unacked data.
                if payload_len == 0
                    if tcp_sockets[s].send_len > 0
                        if tcp_sockets[s].fast_recovery == 0
                            // Count toward fast-retransmit trigger.
                            tcp_sockets[s].dup_ack_count += 1
                            if tcp_sockets[s].dup_ack_count == 3
                                inet_tcp_fast_retransmit(s)
                        else
                            // RFC 5681 §3.2 ¶7 — already in fast
                            // recovery. Each additional dup-ACK
                            // means another segment left the net,
                            // so inflate cwnd by one SMSS and try
                            // to drain (may enable a new segment
                            // to be sent).
                            tcp_sockets[s].snd_cwnd += TCP_MAX_SEG
                            if tcp_sockets[s].snd_cwnd > 65535
                                tcp_sockets[s].snd_cwnd = 65535
                            // Each dup-ACK during recovery likely
                            // brought a fresh SACK block — if a new
                            // hole becomes visible, plug it now.
                            if tcp_sockets[s].sack_ok != 0
                                inet_tcp_sack_rexmit_next(s)
                            inet_tcp_drain_send(s)

        var did_data = 0
        var did_fin = 0
        if payload_len > 0
            if seq == tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt
                val room = tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size - tcp_sockets[s].recv_len
                var take = payload_len
                if take > room
                    take = room
                var i = 0
                while i < take
                    tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr[tcp_sockets[s].recv_len + i] = payload[i]
                    i += 1
                tcp_sockets[s].recv_len += take
                tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt = tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt + u32(take)
                did_data = 1
                // Bytes arrived in-order — fire EPOLLIN. drain_reorder
                // below has its own fire so OOO-then-in-order doesn't
                // miss a wake either.
                if take > 0
                    inet_epoll_fire(2, s)
            else if tcp_seq_lt(tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt, seq) != 0
                // seq > rcv_nxt: out-of-order. Stash if it fits in
                // our advertised window. If the stash fails (full
                // queue, overlap), peer will retransmit — the dup
                // re-ACK path below still advertises rcv_nxt.
                val seg_end = seq + u32(payload_len)
                val rem = tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_size - tcp_sockets[s].recv_len - inet_tcp_reorder_bytes(s)
                var rem_clamped = rem
                if rem_clamped < 0
                    rem_clamped = 0
                val wnd_room = u32(rem_clamped)
                val wnd_end = tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt + wnd_room
                if tcp_seq_le(seg_end, wnd_end) != 0
                    inet_tcp_reorder_store(s, seq, payload, payload_len)

        if (flags & TCP_F_FIN) != 0
            val fin_end = seq + u32(payload_len)
            if fin_end == tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt
                tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt = tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt + u32(1)
                tcp_sockets[s].peer_fin = 1
                did_fin = 1
                // peer_fin transitions readability + hangup — fire so
                // an epoll waiter sees EPOLLIN | EPOLLHUP immediately.
                inet_epoll_fire(2, s)
            else if tcp_seq_lt(tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt, fin_end) != 0
                // FIN is out-of-order — stash its seq and process
                // when drain catches up.
                tcp_sockets[s].fin_seq = fin_end
                tcp_sockets[s].fin_pending = 1

        if did_data != 0
            val drained = inet_tcp_drain_reorder(s)
            if (drained & 2) != 0
                did_fin = 1

        if did_data != 0
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt, u32(0), 0)
            if tcp_sockets[s].recv_waiter >= 0
                val mx = tcp_sockets[s].recv_maxlen
                var give = tcp_sockets[s].recv_len
                if give > mx
                    give = mx
                inet_tcp_reply_recv(s, give)
        if did_fin != 0
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt, u32(0), 0)

        // Re-ACK out-of-window segments (old data or dup FIN peer is
        // retransmitting because an earlier ACK of ours was lost).
        // Without this, peer keeps retransmitting until giveup+RST.
        if did_data == 0 && did_fin == 0
            if payload_len > 0 || (flags & TCP_F_FIN) != 0
                inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK, tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt, u32(0), 0)

        val st2 = tcp_sockets[s].state
        if st2 == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED
            if did_fin != 0
                tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
                if tcp_sockets[s].recv_waiter >= 0
                    if tcp_sockets[s].recv_len == 0
                        inet_tcp_reply_recv(s, 0)
        else if st2 == TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_1
            if tcp_sockets[s].snd_una == tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
                if tcp_sockets[s].peer_fin != 0
                    // Our FIN acked and peer has FIN'd (either in this
                    // segment — simultaneous close — or earlier while
                    // our FIN was still unacked, which RFC 793 models
                    // as the CLOSING state). Either way enter TIME_WAIT.
                    inet_tcp_enter_time_wait(s)
                else
                    // Arm an idle timeout so the slot is reclaimed
                    // even if the peer never FINs. Linux's default
                    // tcp_fin_timeout is 60s; we match that.
                    tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_2
                    tcp_sockets[s].tw_deadline = int(monotonic_ms()) + TCP_FW2_MS
        else if st2 == TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_2
            if did_fin != 0
                // Peer FIN after our FIN-ACK — enter TIME_WAIT.
                inet_tcp_enter_time_wait(s)
        else if st2 == TCP_STATE_LAST_ACK
            if tcp_sockets[s].snd_una == tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt
                inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)


Called from inet main loop on every wake. Scans every active
socket for retransmit and TIME_WAIT deadlines.

    inet_tcp_scan_timers()
        val now = int(monotonic_ms())
        var i = 0
        while i < TCP_MAX_SOCKETS
            if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                val rtx = tcp_sockets[i].rtx_deadline
                if rtx != 0
                    if now >= rtx
                        if tcp_sockets[i].retries >= TCP_MAX_RETRIES
                            tcp_sockets[i].pending_error = 110  // ETIMEDOUT
                            inet_tcp_close_slot(i, -1)
                        else
                            tcp_sockets[i].retries += 1
                            inet_tcp_cwnd_on_rto(i)
                            inet_tcp_retransmit(i)
                            inet_tcp_rto_backoff(i)
                            inet_tcp_arm_rtx(i)
                // SO_SNDTIMEO: parked sender wakes with status=2 once
                // its deadline elapses.  Caller (shim sys_write /
                // sys_sendto_tcp) maps that to -EAGAIN, or returns
                // accumulated `sent_total` if any chunk got through
                // before the deadline.
                if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                    val sd = tcp_sockets[i].send_deadline
                    if sd != 0 && tcp_sockets[i].send_waiter >= 0
                        if now >= sd
                            val w = tcp_sockets[i].send_waiter
                            tcp_sockets[i].send_waiter = -1
                            tcp_sockets[i].send_waiter_len = 0
                            tcp_sockets[i].send_deadline = 0
                            inet_tcp_reply_send(w, 2, 0)
                // TCP_USER_TIMEOUT (RFC 5482) — abort the connection
                // when no progress has been made on unacked data for
                // `user_timeout_ms`.  Distinct from SO_SNDTIMEO:
                // SNDTIMEO returns EAGAIN to the caller and leaves
                // the connection alive; USER_TIMEOUT records a
                // pending ETIMEDOUT and tears the slot down so the
                // *next* read/write/getsockopt(SO_ERROR) surfaces 110
                // (Linux semantics).  The earliest_unack_ms gate
                // ensures we don't fire on idle connections that have
                // simply gone quiet.
                if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                    val ut = tcp_sockets[i].user_timeout_ms
                    val eu = tcp_sockets[i].earliest_unack_ms
                    if ut > 0 && eu > 0
                        if (now - eu) >= ut
                            tcp_sockets[i].pending_error = 110  // ETIMEDOUT
                            inet_tcp_close_slot(i, -1)
                val tw = tcp_sockets[i].tw_deadline
                if tw != 0
                    if now >= tw
                        inet_tcp_close_slot(i, 0)
                // SO_KEEPALIVE — only fires on ESTABLISHED /
                // CLOSE_WAIT (real connections with a peer to probe).
                // SYN_SENT / SYN_RCVD have their own retransmit
                // timer; LISTEN / TIME_WAIT have nothing to keep alive.
                if tcp_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                    if tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_on != 0
                        val kst = tcp_sockets[i].state
                        if kst == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED || kst == TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
                            var idle_eff = 0
                            var intvl_eff = 0
                            var cnt_eff = 0
                            inet_tcp_eff_keepalive(i, idle_eff, intvl_eff, cnt_eff)
                            if tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_next_probe == 0
                                // Idle timer just (re)started — schedule
                                // first probe `idle_eff` ticks after last recv.
                                val first_probe = tcp_sockets[i].last_recv_ms + idle_eff
                                tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_next_probe = first_probe
                            if now >= tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_next_probe
                                if tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_probes >= cnt_eff
                                    // Peer is dead. Close as -ETIMEDOUT.
                                    tcp_sockets[i].pending_error = 110  // ETIMEDOUT
                                    inet_tcp_close_slot(i, -1)
                                else
                                    // Probe: empty ACK at snd_una - 1
                                    // (Linux's "1 byte before unacked"
                                    // pattern). Peer ACKs back with the
                                    // current ack number, which our
                                    // handle_segment treats as fresh
                                    // liveness and resets probes.
                                    val probe_seq = tcp_sockets[i].snd_una - u32(1)
                                    inet_tcp_emit(i, TCP_F_ACK, probe_seq, u32(0), 0)
                                    tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_probes += 1
                                    tcp_sockets[i].keepalive_next_probe = now + intvl_eff
            i += 1

    inet_tcp_retransmit(s: int)
        val st = tcp_sockets[s].state
        if st == TCP_STATE_SYN_SENT
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_SYN, tcp_sockets[s].iss, u32(0), 0)
            return
        if st == TCP_STATE_SYN_RCVD
            inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_SYN | TCP_F_ACK, tcp_sockets[s].iss, u32(0), 0)
            return
        if st == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED || st == TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
            if tcp_sockets[s].send_len > 0
                // Retransmit exactly one MSS-sized segment from the
                // front of send_buf. cwnd has already been collapsed
                // to TCP_MIN_CWND in scan_timers; subsequent ACKs
                // will grow it back via slow start and drain_send
                // will push more segments as the window opens.
                var retx_len = tcp_sockets[s].send_len
                if retx_len > TCP_MAX_SEG
                    retx_len = TCP_MAX_SEG
                val pmtu_cap = inet_tcp_eff_mss(s)
                if retx_len > pmtu_cap
                    retx_len = pmtu_cap
                inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_PSH,
                    tcp_sockets[s].snd_una, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una,
                    retx_len)
                val retx_end = tcp_sockets[s].snd_una + u32(retx_len)
                tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt = retx_end
            return
        if st == TCP_STATE_FIN_WAIT_1
            // If data was still in flight when close() ran, resend it
            // together with the FIN. A FIN-only retransmit leaves the
            // peer rejecting our FIN as out-of-window until giveup.
            if tcp_sockets[s].send_len > 0
                inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_FIN | TCP_F_PSH,
                    tcp_sockets[s].snd_una, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una,
                    tcp_sockets[s].send_len)
            else
                inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_FIN,
                    tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt - u32(1), u32(0), 0)
            return
        if st == TCP_STATE_LAST_ACK
            if tcp_sockets[s].send_len > 0
                inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_FIN | TCP_F_PSH,
                    tcp_sockets[s].snd_una, tcp_sockets[s].snd_una,
                    tcp_sockets[s].send_len)
            else
                inet_tcp_emit(s, TCP_F_ACK | TCP_F_FIN,
                    tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt - u32(1), u32(0), 0)
            return


Test-only: drive inet_tcp_state_step against a scratch TCP socket
with a deliberately out-of-order segment sequence. Verifies stash,
drain, and recv_buf ordering. Returns 0 on pass, 1..6 on failure.

Sequence exercised:
  1. Seg A at seq 200 len 50  (bytes 'B') — out-of-order, stashed.
  2. Seg B at seq 250 len 50  (bytes 'C') — out-of-order, stashed.
  3. Seg C at seq 100 len 100 (bytes 'A') — in-order, triggers
     drain of the two stashed slots so rcv_nxt advances 100 → 300.
Expected final state: rcv_nxt = 300, recv_len = 200, recv_buf =
100 'A' + 50 'B' + 50 'C'.

    inet_tcp_ooo_selftest() -> int
        // Use inet_tcp_alloc so the heap-backed send/recv buffers
        // exist — the in-order seg below copies its payload through
        // recv_buf_ptr, which is null on a manually-initialised slot.
        val s = inet_tcp_alloc()
        if s < 0
            return 100
        tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED
        tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt = u32(100)
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_una = u32(1)
        tcp_sockets[s].snd_nxt = u32(1)
        inet_tcp_owners_init(s)
        tcp_sockets[s].local_port = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].remote_port = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip = u32(0)

        var seg_b: [50]byte
        var i = 0
        while i < 50
            seg_b[i] = byte('B')
            i += 1
        inet_tcp_state_step(s, TCP_F_ACK, u32(200), u32(1), 1024,
            seg_b, 50, 0, 0, 0, 0, u32(0), u32(0))
        if tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt != u32(100)
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 1

        var seg_c: [50]byte
        i = 0
        while i < 50
            seg_c[i] = byte('C')
            i += 1
        inet_tcp_state_step(s, TCP_F_ACK, u32(250), u32(1), 1024,
            seg_c, 50, 0, 0, 0, 0, u32(0), u32(0))
        if tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt != u32(100)
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 2

        // Two segments totalling 100 bytes are stashed — helper must
        // report 100 so the advertised rwnd can shrink accordingly.
        if inet_tcp_reorder_bytes(s) != 100
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 13

        var seg_a: [100]byte
        i = 0
        while i < 100
            seg_a[i] = byte('A')
            i += 1
        inet_tcp_state_step(s, TCP_F_ACK, u32(100), u32(1), 1024,
            seg_a, 100, 0, 0, 0, 0, u32(0), u32(0))
        if tcp_sockets[s].rcv_nxt != u32(300)
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 3
        if tcp_sockets[s].recv_len != 200
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 4

        i = 0
        while i < 100
            if tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr[i] != byte('A')
                inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
                return 5
            i += 1
        while i < 150
            if tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr[i] != byte('B')
                inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
                return 5
            i += 1
        while i < 200
            if tcp_sockets[s].recv_buf_ptr[i] != byte('C')
                inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
                return 5
            i += 1

        // After drain the reorder queue is empty — helper must
        // return 0 so rwnd is only shrunk while bytes are in flight.
        if inet_tcp_reorder_bytes(s) != 0
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 14

        // RFC 6298 RTO math. Scratch socket starts fresh in alloc
        // state (rtt_measured = 0). We overwrote the above fields
        // directly instead of going through alloc, so rebaseline
        // the RTT state first.
        tcp_sockets[s].srtt = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].rttvar = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].rto = TCP_RTO_MS_INITIAL
        tcp_sockets[s].rtt_measured = 0
        tcp_sockets[s].rtt_sample_valid = 0

        // First measurement R=500 ms → SRTT=500, RTTVAR=250, RTO=1500.
        // Above TCP_RTO_MS_MIN (200) and well below TCP_RTO_MS_MAX (60000),
        // so no clamping fires.
        inet_tcp_rtt_update(s, 500)
        if tcp_sockets[s].srtt != 500
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 6
        if tcp_sockets[s].rttvar != 250
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 7
        if tcp_sockets[s].rto != 1500
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 8

        // Second measurement R=400.
        //   RTTVAR = 250 + (|500-400| - 250) >> 2 = 250 + (-150 >> 2)
        //          = 250 + (-38) = 212
        //   SRTT   = 500 + (400-500) >> 3         = 500 + (-100 >> 3)
        //          = 500 + (-13) = 487
        //   RTO    = 487 + 4*212 = 487 + 848 = 1335
        inet_tcp_rtt_update(s, 400)
        if tcp_sockets[s].srtt != 487
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 9
        if tcp_sockets[s].rttvar != 212
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 10
        if tcp_sockets[s].rto != 1335
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 11

        // Backoff doubles RTO: 1335 → 2670 (still below MAX=60000).
        inet_tcp_rto_backoff(s)
        if tcp_sockets[s].rto != 2670
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 12

        inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)

        // IP-config round-trip: save the current triple, install a
        // deliberately different one, verify every getter + the
        // bitwise `inet_in_subnet`, then restore. Lets the harness
        // prove inet_set/get + CIDR matching before a real DHCP
        // client is live.
        val saved_ip = inet_get_our_ip()
        val saved_mask = inet_get_mask()
        val saved_gw = inet_get_gw()
        val probe_ip = ipv4(192, 168, 7, 42)
        val probe_mask = ipv4(255, 255, 0, 0)
        val probe_gw = ipv4(192, 168, 7, 1)
        inet_set_ip_config(probe_ip, probe_mask, probe_gw)
        if inet_get_our_ip() != probe_ip
            inet_set_ip_config(saved_ip, saved_mask, saved_gw)
            return 15
        if inet_get_mask() != probe_mask
            inet_set_ip_config(saved_ip, saved_mask, saved_gw)
            return 16
        if inet_get_gw() != probe_gw
            inet_set_ip_config(saved_ip, saved_mask, saved_gw)
            return 17
        // /16 says 192.168.99.5 is on-link; 192.169.0.5 is not.
        if inet_in_subnet(ipv4(192, 168, 99, 5)) == 0
            inet_set_ip_config(saved_ip, saved_mask, saved_gw)
            return 18
        if inet_in_subnet(ipv4(192, 169, 0, 5)) != 0
            inet_set_ip_config(saved_ip, saved_mask, saved_gw)
            return 19
        inet_set_ip_config(saved_ip, saved_mask, saved_gw)
        if inet_get_our_ip() != saved_ip
            return 20

        // RFC 2018 SACK-permitted parser. QEMU's slirp doesn't carry
        // the option in SYN-ACKs, so we can't observe negotiation
        // over the wire in CI — verify it in isolation instead.
        //
        // Frame layout: 14 B Ethernet header + 20 B IPv4 header +
        // 28 B TCP header (MSS + SACK-permitted + 2 NOPs).
        var synframe: [54]byte
        var bi = 0
        while bi < 54
            synframe[bi] = 0
            bi += 1
        // Data offset = 7 words (28 bytes); flags = SYN.
        synframe[34 + 12] = byte(0x70)
        synframe[34 + 13] = byte(TCP_F_SYN & 0xff)
        synframe[34 + 20] = byte(TCP_OPT_MSS)
        synframe[34 + 21] = 4
        synframe[34 + 22] = byte(0x05)
        synframe[34 + 23] = byte(0xb4)
        synframe[34 + 24] = byte(TCP_OPT_SACK_PERMITTED)
        synframe[34 + 25] = 2
        synframe[34 + 26] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
        synframe[34 + 27] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
        if tcp_opts_has_sack_permitted(synframe, 34, 28) != 1
            return 21

        // Same frame without SACK-permitted (replace kind=4 with a
        // 2-byte unknown option) must parse as "not permitted".
        synframe[34 + 24] = byte(0xfe)
        if tcp_opts_has_sack_permitted(synframe, 34, 28) != 0
            return 22

        // Exercise the SYN_SENT → ESTABLISHED transition with a
        // synthetic peer_sack_perm = 1 and verify `sack_ok` flips.
        val ss = TCP_MAX_SOCKETS - 1
        tcp_sockets[ss].in_use = 1
        tcp_sockets[ss].state = TCP_STATE_SYN_SENT
        tcp_sockets[ss].snd_una = u32(0)
        tcp_sockets[ss].snd_nxt = u32(1)
        tcp_sockets[ss].recv_len = 0
        tcp_sockets[ss].send_len = 0
        tcp_sockets[ss].fin_pending = 0
        tcp_sockets[ss].peer_fin = 0
        tcp_sockets[ss].our_fin_sent = 0
        tcp_sockets[ss].recv_waiter = -1
        tcp_sockets[ss].send_waiter = -1
        tcp_sockets[ss].connect_waiter = -1
        tcp_sockets[ss].close_waiter = -1
        tcp_sockets[ss].accept_waiter = -1
        tcp_sockets[ss].is_listen = 0
        inet_tcp_owners_init(ss)
        tcp_sockets[ss].local_port = 0
        tcp_sockets[ss].remote_port = 0
        tcp_sockets[ss].remote_ip = u32(0)
        tcp_sockets[ss].sack_ok = 0
        var rk = 0
        while rk < TCP_REORDER_SLOTS
            tcp_sockets[ss].reorder[rk].in_use = 0
            rk += 1
        // state_step only inspects flags/seq/ack/wnd + peer_sack_perm
        // when walking the SYN_SENT → ESTABLISHED edge — the payload
        // pointer is unused for the SYN-ACK case, so pass a stub.
        var nullbuf: [1]byte
        inet_tcp_state_step(ss,
            TCP_F_SYN | TCP_F_ACK,
            u32(100), u32(1), 1024,
            nullbuf, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, u32(0), u32(0))
        if tcp_sockets[ss].sack_ok != 1
            inet_tcp_close_slot(ss, 0)
            return 23
        inet_tcp_close_slot(ss, 0)

        // Phase 4b sender scoreboard. Exercise the primitives
        // the live selective-retransmit path is built on —
        // QEMU's slirp strips SACK options so we can't drive
        // these through a real peer; verify them in isolation.
        val sb = TCP_MAX_SOCKETS - 1
        tcp_sockets[sb].in_use = 1
        tcp_sockets[sb].snd_una = u32(1000)
        tcp_sockets[sb].snd_nxt = u32(4360)
        var bc = 0
        while bc < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].in_use = 0
            bc += 1

        // Single block `[2440, 3400)` — a hole under it at
        // 1000..2440 and another at 3400..4360. `next_hole`
        // should report 1000 first, then 3400 if the cursor
        // has already consumed the first hole.
        tcp_sack_board_insert(sb, u32(2440), u32(3400))
        if tcp_sack_next_hole(sb, u32(1000)) != u32(1000)
            inet_tcp_close_slot(sb, 0)
            return 24
        if tcp_sack_next_hole(sb, u32(2440)) != u32(3400)
            inet_tcp_close_slot(sb, 0)
            return 25
        if tcp_sack_next_hole(sb, u32(3400)) != u32(3400)
            inet_tcp_close_slot(sb, 0)
            return 26

        // Adjacent block `[3400, 3880)` — must coalesce with the
        // existing one into a single `[2440, 3880)` entry so the
        // scoreboard stays compact under heavy SACK churn.
        tcp_sack_board_insert(sb, u32(3400), u32(3880))
        var merged_seen = 0
        var any_other = 0
        bc = 0
        while bc < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].in_use != 0
                if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].left == u32(2440)
                    if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].right == u32(3880)
                        merged_seen = 1
                    else
                        any_other = 1
                else
                    any_other = 1
            bc += 1
        if merged_seen == 0
            inet_tcp_close_slot(sb, 0)
            return 27
        if any_other != 0
            inet_tcp_close_slot(sb, 0)
            return 28

        // Advance snd_una past the block's left edge but not its
        // right. Purge must clip the block's left edge so the
        // scoreboard only covers bytes still-in-flight.
        tcp_sockets[sb].snd_una = u32(3000)
        tcp_sack_board_purge(sb)
        var clipped_seen = 0
        bc = 0
        while bc < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].in_use != 0
                if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].left == u32(3000)
                    if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].right == u32(3880)
                        clipped_seen = 1
            bc += 1
        if clipped_seen == 0
            inet_tcp_close_slot(sb, 0)
            return 29

        // Advance snd_una past the block's right edge. Purge
        // must drop it entirely — no stale entry to mislead
        // `next_hole` on the next recovery round.
        tcp_sockets[sb].snd_una = u32(3880)
        tcp_sack_board_purge(sb)
        bc = 0
        while bc < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].in_use != 0
                inet_tcp_close_slot(sb, 0)
                return 30
            bc += 1

        // Drive the byte-walk that `tcp_opts_apply_sack`
        // uses on the wire. Synthesize a TCP header with two
        // SACK blocks — `[500, 700)` and `[1200, 1500)` —
        // under a reset scoreboard, parse, verify both blocks
        // landed as distinct `[left, right)` pairs. The header
        // layout mirrors what a typical Linux dup-ACK would send:
        // NOP + NOP + kind=5 + len=18 + 2 blocks.
        tcp_sack_board_clear(sb)
        tcp_sockets[sb].snd_una = u32(400)
        var ackframe: [60]byte
        var ai = 0
        while ai < 60
            ackframe[ai] = 0
            ai += 1
        // Data offset = 10 words (40 bytes header); flags = ACK.
        ackframe[34 + 12] = byte(0xa0)
        ackframe[34 + 13] = byte(TCP_F_ACK & 0xff)
        ackframe[34 + 20] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
        ackframe[34 + 21] = byte(TCP_OPT_NOP)
        ackframe[34 + 22] = byte(TCP_OPT_SACK)
        ackframe[34 + 23] = byte(2 + 8 * 2)
        // Block 1: left=500, right=700.
        ackframe[34 + 24] = 0
        ackframe[34 + 25] = 0
        ackframe[34 + 26] = byte(1)
        ackframe[34 + 27] = byte(244)
        ackframe[34 + 28] = 0
        ackframe[34 + 29] = 0
        ackframe[34 + 30] = byte(2)
        ackframe[34 + 31] = byte(188)
        // Block 2: left=1200, right=1500.
        ackframe[34 + 32] = 0
        ackframe[34 + 33] = 0
        ackframe[34 + 34] = byte(4)
        ackframe[34 + 35] = byte(176)
        ackframe[34 + 36] = 0
        ackframe[34 + 37] = 0
        ackframe[34 + 38] = byte(5)
        ackframe[34 + 39] = byte(220)
        tcp_opts_apply_sack(sb, ackframe, 34, 40)
        var saw_500 = 0
        var saw_1200 = 0
        bc = 0
        while bc < TCP_SACK_BOARD
            if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].in_use != 0
                if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].left == u32(500)
                    if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].right == u32(700)
                        saw_500 = 1
                if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].left == u32(1200)
                    if tcp_sockets[sb].sack_board[bc].right == u32(1500)
                        saw_1200 = 1
            bc += 1
        if saw_500 == 0
            inet_tcp_close_slot(sb, 0)
            return 31
        if saw_1200 == 0
            inet_tcp_close_slot(sb, 0)
            return 32
        inet_tcp_close_slot(sb, 0)

        0


Test-only: prove the send-buf parking pipeline works end to end.

Steps:
  1. Allocate a TCP slot, force ESTABLISHED.
  2. Pre-fill `send_buf` to capacity so `inet_tcp_do_send` returns
     0 (the trigger condition for parking).
  3. Verify `send_waiter` is unset (-1) initially.
  4. Synthetically park a fake waiter (a tid past `thread_count`
     so `ipc_reply_handler`'s validation drops the wake silently).
  5. Drain `send_len` to mimic an incoming ACK.
  6. Call `inet_tcp_drain_send_waiter`.  It must clear
     `send_waiter` to -1 because space > 0 after the drain.
  7. Run it again — the second call is a no-op (waiter already
     cleared) and must not crash.

Returns 0 on full pass; 1..6 on the failure step.

Test-only: prove the ARP-driven TCP retransmit hook works.

  1. Pin a deterministic IP config (10.0.2.15/24 with gw
     10.0.2.2) so the next-hop math is predictable, regardless of
     whether DHCP has run.
  2. Allocate a TCP slot, force ESTABLISHED, point it at an IP
     in subnet (10.0.2.99) so its next-hop is itself, not the
     gateway.
  3. Set `arp_pending = 1` directly — the same flag that
     `inet_tcp_emit` sets on a real MAC miss.
  4. Insert a fake ARP entry for 10.0.2.99 via `inet_arp_insert`,
     which fires `inet_arp_drain_pending` at the end.  The drain
     walks TCP, finds our slot with `arp_pending=1`, clears it,
     and calls `inet_tcp_retransmit`.
  5. Verify `arp_pending == 0` afterwards.

Returns 0 on pass, 1..3 on the failure step.

    inet_tcp_arp_replay_selftest() -> int
        val saved_ip = inet_get_our_ip()
        val saved_mask = inet_get_mask()
        val saved_gw = inet_get_gw()
        inet_set_ip_config(ipv4(10, 0, 2, 15), ipv4(255, 255, 255, 0),
            ipv4(10, 0, 2, 2))

        val s = inet_tcp_alloc()
        if s < 0
            inet_set_ip_config(saved_ip, saved_mask, saved_gw)
            return 1
        tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED
        val test_ip = ipv4(10, 0, 2, 99)
        tcp_sockets[s].remote_ip = test_ip
        tcp_sockets[s].arp_pending = 1

        var fake_mac: [6]byte
        var i = 0
        while i < 6
            fake_mac[i] = byte(0x10 + i)
            i += 1
        inet_arp_insert(test_ip, fake_mac)

        val rc = if tcp_sockets[s].arp_pending == 0 then 0 else 2

        val idx = inet_arp_lookup_idx(test_ip)
        if idx >= 0
            inet_arp_cache[idx].in_use = 0
        inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
        inet_set_ip_config(saved_ip, saved_mask, saved_gw)
        rc


    inet_tcp_send_park_selftest() -> int
        val s = inet_tcp_alloc()
        if s < 0
            return 1
        tcp_sockets[s].state = TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED

        if tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter != -1
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 2

        tcp_sockets[s].send_len = tcp_sockets[s].send_buf_size
        var d: [1]byte
        d[0] = byte('q')
        val ds = inet_tcp_do_send(s, &d[0], 1)
        if ds != 0
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 3

        // Fake tid past thread_count — ipc_reply_handler drops it.
        tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter = 0xCAFE
        tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter_len = 1

        tcp_sockets[s].send_len = 0
        inet_tcp_drain_send_waiter(s)

        if tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter != -1
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 4
        if tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter_len != 0
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 5

        // Second call is a no-op: waiter already cleared.
        inet_tcp_drain_send_waiter(s)
        if tcp_sockets[s].send_waiter != -1
            inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
            return 6

        inet_tcp_close_slot(s, 0)
        0
